\section{Related works}
\label{headings}


There is an increasing body of literature on applying LLMs to generate HLS design from original C code. C2HLS \cite{collini2024c2hlsc} evaluates the basic task of translating naive C++ into synthesizable HLS C++. \cite{bhattacharyya2024llm} demonstrates that LLMs can automate HLS pragmas and optimizations to produce synthesizable, high-performance RTL from C on image-processing benchmarks. LIFT \cite{prakriya2025lift} introduces LLM–GNN hybrid methods that automatically optimize C/C++ designs to boost FPGA performance. \cite{xu2024automated} presents an LLM-driven HLS program-repair framework that transforms C/C++ into synthesizable HLS-C. HLSPilot \cite{xiong2024hlspilot} extends this approach with retrieval-augmented generation and chain-of-thought prompting to deliver optimized HLS implementations across nine applications. However, to date, no work has established a large-scale dataset for thoroughly evaluating an LLM's HLS capabilities.

First, prior HLS datasets have primarily focused on resource estimation rather than on generating HLS code from C, and available program sources remain scarce. HLSsyn \cite{bai2023towards} concentrates on encompassing a diverse range of optimization pragmas into only 42 kernels for training and evaluation of design quality prediction models. HLSDataset \cite{wei2023hlsdataset}, including 34 data sources and 18K samples, targets power estimates, resource, and timing estimation. MLSBench \cite{goswami2022mlsbench} is an open-source dataset created with the Xilinx Vivado HLS flow, covering 17 C/C++ and 13 SystemC benchmarks drawn from Polybench, CHStone, and MachSuite. It includes only HLS log files and reports. Spector \cite{gautier2016spector} comprises a collection of OpenCL applications that the authors process with the Intel OpenCL SDK to produce 8,300 distinct hardware implementations for Intel FPGAs. Dai et al. \cite{dai2018fast} have open-sourced a dataset of about 1,300 designs generated by using applications from suites such as CHStone, Machsuite, and Rosetta. Second, existing datasets inadequately capture comprehensive C-to-HLS transformations, focusing largely on pragma insertion. Third, they are not organized as paired C-and-HLS examples and omit the corresponding testbenches needed for LLM-based HLS code optimization, which are not ready for LLM.

Compared with previous works, Table \ref{tab:ml-datasets-comparison} concludes that our dataset has more kinds of sources and supports more transformations, making it ready for LLM code generation.



