\section{High-Level Synthesis (HLS) Datasets Construction}
Open-source HLS datasets are scarce and unstructured, making them insufficient for training LLMs. To address this, we will develop an automated pipeline that generates high-quality HLS datasets from the limited available resources. The datasets are released under the MG0-2.0 license~\citep{duan2024modelgo}.\footnote{\url{https://www.modelgo.li/}}. To construct a high-quality dataset, we need two steps. The first step is to collect high-quality open-source HLS examples. However, human-optimized code examples are not enough. The second step is the effective data augmentation for human-optimized codes to produce effective codes. Figure \ref{fig:datagen} shows our dataset construction process. 

\subsection{Dataset Collection}
Manually optimized codebases often suffer from inconsistent formatting and minimal documentation, making them ill-suited for LLM-driven code generation. Public kernels frequently rely on unexpanded macros and include extraneous utility functions that obscure core algorithms. Our goal is to prepare C-to-HLS designs for seamless LLM consumption by packaging each design with the following files:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1em]  % set a single, small indent
  \item Single original file \(x\) that is a slow original C/C++ codes.
  \item Single optimized HLS file \(y\) that implements the kernel, including a top function and, if necessary, any sub-functions and specialized data types. The file must be synthesizable and not exceed the resources of the platform.
  \item Self-contained C++ testbench \(tb\) includes all test cases and validation logic necessary to verify the kernel’s outputs against expected results. We manually write the testbenches and adjust the optimized HLS code to ensure it passes all tests.

\end{itemize}

Therefore, we construct pairs \((x,y,tb)\). If the original file \(x\) is synthesisable, the execution cycles from synthesis reports of \(y\) must be less than \(y\). If the original file \(x\) is not synthesisable, \(y\) should be synthesisable. Table \ref{tab:data-sources-summary} shows our HLS dataset collection including common heterogeneous benchmarks such as polybench, and other FPGA benchmarks.

