\subsection{Dataset Augmentation}
\label{sec:datasetaug}
Given the performance, resource utilization, and compilation feedback provided by EDA toolchains, we employ Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) and design space exploration (DSE) tools to efficiently explore the large and combinatorial action space as shown in Figure \ref{fig:datagen}. 

First, MCTS performs structured exploration by balancing the exploitation of high-reward actions with the exploration of uncertain or less-visited regions of the search space. The optimization policy is generated by the retrieval augmentation generation (RAG) module. The search is guided to choose the suitable policy by both the verification pipeline and a reward model. The reward model incorporates detailed feedback from the HLS toolchain, including synthesis success or failure, compile warnings, and performance metrics such as resource usage, latency, and throughput. This heuristic-driven strategy enables the agent to iteratively refine transformation sequences and produce more high-quality, synthesizable HLS designs. 

Second, HLS directive design space exploration using genetic algorithms \cite{ferikoglou2023collectivehls} is adopted. It inserts pipeline, unroll, and partition pragmas to produce more effective data samples. Through iterative refinement, the framework converges toward optimized and synthesizable HLS code. After collecting these C-to-HLS code pairs, we use an LLM to produce a chain-of-thought narrative that analyzes the performance and resource bottlenecks, explains why the original code cannot be synthesized, and highlights the key optimizations applied in the HLS version. This explanatory reasoning is then integrated alongside the optimized HLS output.



