\textbf{\textit{State S:}} The state \(S_t\) at time step \(t\) is defined as the current version of the HLS code after applying the previous actions.

\textbf{\textit{Reward R:}} Rule‐based reward shaping has proven effective in guiding agent behavior in previous work \cite{guo2025deepseek}. In our framework, the reward function \(R(s_t, a_t)\) is computed by applying rule‐based scoring to verification results and feedback provided by the HLS tool. A penalty of \(-2\) is applied when the verification fails, and \(-1\) if synthesis fails or the design exceeds resource constraints. A neutral reward of $0$ is given when the transformed design performs worse than the original, while a reward of $1$ is granted when it performs better. If the design not only surpasses the original but also meets timing constraints, a higher reward of $2$ is assigned.



In our cases, the MCTS begins at the initial state \(S_0\) (the root node), which is the naive HLS code. From a state \(S_t\), the agent applies an optimization policy \(\pi\), i.e., an action \(a_t \in \mathcal{A}\), transitioning to the subsequent state \(S_{t+1}\). MCTS consists of four key phases: selection, expansion, simulation, and backpropagation. 

\textbf{Selection}: We employ the upper confidence bounds for trees (UCT) \cite{gelly2006exploration} algorithm to choose nodes.\[
\pi(s_t) 
= \arg\max_{a_t \in \mathcal{A}} 
\left(
\underbrace{R(s_t, a_t)}_{\text{reward}}
+ \beta \times
\underbrace{\frac{\sqrt{1 + N(s_t)}}{1 + N(s_t, a_t)}}_{\text{ \(U\) Term}}
\right).
\]

\textbf{Expansion:} From the current state \(s_t\), generate one or more child nodes to explore untried actions, formalized as \( a_{t+1} = \mathrm{llm}(s_t)\).

\textbf{Simulation:} Perform a rollout from the chosen child node by applying \(a_{t+1}\), running HLS synthesis to estimate latency and resource utilization, and computing the reward \( R(s_t, a_{t+1})\).

\textbf{Backpropagation:} Propagate the obtained reward back up the visited path, updating each node’s statistics (e.g., visit count and value estimate) to improve future selection.

\subsubsection{Design Space Exploration}
The tool implements an automated HLS design-space explorer that uses a genetic-algorithm optimizer to discover effective directive combinations, specifically loop pipelining, loop unrolling, and array partitioning, that maximize performance and resource utilization. By encoding each candidate solution as a set of directive parameters, evolving them through mutation and crossover, and evaluating them via HLS synthesis feedback, the tool generates high-quality configurations without manual tuning.

Overall, we leverage DeepSeek-R1 to generate high-quality synthetic code examples, the AMD Vitis HLS EDA tool, and DSE tools to validate, annotate, and collect performance/resource metrics within our framework, yielding an effective, end-to-end HLS code dataset. The detailed information on dataset generation is in Appendix \ref{app:datasetgen}.


\section{Experimental Evaluation of LLMs on Our Dataset}

\subsection{Prompting Methods}

\textbf{Zero-shot Prompting:} We craft concise, HLS-specific prompts that instruct the model to perform code optimization or transformation from its pretrained knowledge, without any additional fine-tuning or example demonstrations \cite{liu2021makes} \cite{wei2021finetuned}.

\textbf{Chain-of-Thought Prompting:} Building on the chain-of-thought approach of Wei et al. \cite{wei2022chain}, our prompts first guide the model through a transformation reasoning phase before asking it to emit the refined code.

\textbf{Retrieval-Based Prompting:} 
Recent studies \cite{shrivastava2023repository} \cite{shypula2023learning} have shown that retrieval-based techniques can substantially boost code generation quality in large language models. In our approach, we first encode each program using CodeBERT \cite{zhou2023codebertscore} to produce rich, semantically informed embeddings. We then index these vectors with FAISS \cite{johnson2019billion} (Facebook AI Similarity Search) and perform a K-nearest-neighbors lookup to retrieve the top K most similar code snippets from our training corpus. Finally, we supply these retrieved examples alongside the original code as additional context to the LLM, guiding it to produce more accurate and effective edits. In our experiments, we set K to 1. The detailed prompt information is in Appendix \ref{app:prompt}.



\subsection{Experiment setting}

\textbf{Fine tuning setting. } Training was conducted using 2 NVIDIA H100 GPUs, each with 80 GB of memory. The computing environment was configured with CUDA 12.2 and cuDNN 9.1 to ensure optimal deep learning performance.


\textbf{Optimization Conditioned Fine-tuning:} Programs that solve the same task can vary widely in how well they perform because of many different kinds of directives and code transformations. The optimized code has a jumble of small or large speedups, without any signal about which gains are most significant. To give the model clearer guidance, we borrow ideas from \cite{shypula2023learning} and attach a “performance tag” and "resource tag" to each solution during training. Each tag reflects how close that program comes to the best attainable performance with resources minimized, using a discrete scale from 1 to 10, respectively. Concretely, for every benchmark problem, we rank its optimized solutions by latency and resource, labeling the top 10\% in performance as a “10/10” performance tag and the lowest 10\% in resource usage as a “10/10” resource tag. By examining these tags, the model learns which code patterns tend to yield the highest performance. At inference time, we prompt the model to generate the best-performing codes with the least resources. The detailed fine-tuning information is in Appendix \ref{app:fine tuning}. 

\textbf{Models.} We evaluate closed-source models, GPT-4o and deepseek-R1. We also used pretrained checkpoints of open-source Qwen models \cite{yang2024qwen2}: Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct, and Qwen2.5-Coder-3B-Instruct obtained via HuggingFace \cite{wolf2020transformers}. 

\textbf{Metrics. }To evaluate the effects of HLS code generations, we use the following metrics:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1em]

\item Functional Accuracy (\%Function Correct): The share of test programs that preserve the original functionality testbench.

\item Synthesis Accuracy (\%Synthesis Correct): The share of programs that can be synthesized.

\item Reduce Latency (Acceleration): The ratio of original latency to new latency, \(accel = o/n\), where \(o\) is the old latency and \(n\) is the new latency from reports of HLS synthesis respectively. We report the minimum, average, and maximum acceleration only for the functionally and syntactically correct cases.

\item Optimization Rate (\%OPT): The percentage of test programs that reduce latency (Acceleration > 1) while remaining functionally correct.

% \item Resource (FF, BRAM, LUT, DSP):  
% The number of flip‑flops (FF), block RAMs (BRAM), lookup tables (LUT), and DSP slices consumed. We calculate their average resource consumption among applications which are function correct and synthesis correct. 

\end{itemize}


\textbf{FPGA Flow Setting:} We transform the given sequential C++ code of these benchmarks into optimized HLS kernel code. We adopt the Vitis HLS design flow, using the Xilinx Alveo U55C data center accelerator card. The target frequency is 200 MHz.

We selected 17 applications as test cases both unsynthesizable designs requiring repair and synthesizable designs in need of optimization and used the remaining 120 applications for training (90\%) and validation (10\%). Previous works \cite{li2022competition} show that generating multiple program candidates per input and selecting the optimal one improves code synthesis performance. We generate \(k\) program variants for each input, then select the fastest one that successfully passes all test cases; we refer to this sampling-and-selection strategy as \(Best@k\).

