We now detail experiments that we conduct to validate our approach on real student response data. 

\subsection{Dataset}

We conduct our experiments on a large real-world dataset of student responses to open-ended short answer items, administered to tens of thousands of students in the United States, containing around $85$K responses to $49$ items from around $63$K students. 
%\nsf{leverage say idea is still generic though shines in this setting, move beginning of meth to here}
%\ml{this stuff belongs till later when you discuss the dataset}
These open-ended items assess reading comprehension skills including argumentation, explanation, and narration, at the grade $6$ level with the average length of student responses being $92$ tokens. Each response is manually scored by a trained human scorer, using a rubric with detailed scoring criteria, with scores in the set $\{0, 1, 2\}$. We show an example item in Table~\ref{tab:qual_ex_studentswarm}. We work in a \textit{low resource setting with few training items}, which is particularly challenging for existing state-of-the-art difficulty prediction methods~\cite{alkhuzaey2024text,benedetto2023quantitative,benedetto2023survey}, which finetune encoder-based LLMs that rely on a large number of training items. 

We split the dataset into $5$ cross-validation folds, each with $29$/$10$/$10$ items in train/validation/test, respectively. We fit IRT models (GPCM) on each fold to estimate ground truth student ability and item parameters.
For each fold, to ensure no information from unseen items is leaked at train time, we fit three separate IRT models, one on the train data, one on train plus validation data, and one on all the data. 
We use the estimated parameters from the three models to train our student simulator, to perform validation, and to perform test-time evaluation, respectively.
We ensure that the distribution of difficulties of items across all splits and folds is roughly equal. We provide further details on dataset processing in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-dataset}.


