\subsection{Cross-chain Connectivity Ecosystem.} Blockchain interoperability has created a dense connectivity ecosystem of bridges linking most Layer-1 and Layer-2 networks. For example, the \textit{Wormhole} protocol alone connects 23+ blockchains across six different smart contract runtimes, and the Axelar network bridges over 40 chains. Likewise, the Cosmos Interchain ecosystem comprises 115+ sovereign chains natively interconnected via the IBC protocol. 
In parallel, Ethereum serves as a de facto hub in this web; many chains deploy bridges to Ethereum to tap its liquidity and user base. Even previously siloed platforms are integrating; for instance, Cardano has explored~\cite{cardano2024} the IBC Protocol~\cite{cosmosIBC2024} to join the Cosmos network. Major Ethereum rollups (e.g., Arbitrum and Optimism) use native bridges to Ethereum. In aggregate, a mesh of bridges now enables tokens and data to move across L1s and L2s, albeit with varying trust assumptions and technical designs. 

\noindent\textbf{Trust Models}:  Classic examples of trustless bridges are \textit{IBC} and Ethereum rollup bridges such as Arbitrum. IBC channels have \textquote{native protocol security}; light clients on each chain validate each other’s consensus state, so \textit{IBC}’s security reduces to the underlying chains’ security with no additional trusted parties. Similarly, rollup-based bridges between Ethereum and its L2 rollups approach trustlessness: a zero-knowledge rollup bridge includes a validity proof with every state update, so Ethereum only accepts withdrawals with a valid proof; an optimistic rollup bridge relies on fraud proofs with a challenge period. In these designs, the bridge inherits the source chain’s security and does not introduce new trust assumptions, aside from at least one honest watcher in optimistic systems. Indeed, industry analyses consider Ethereum’s rollup bridges among the most trustless interoperability solutions available~\cite{kiepuszewski2022rollups}. 

Most cross-chain connectivity today, however, relies on trust-minimized intermediaries rather than fully light-client-based verification. In a trust-minimized bridge, a set of independent validators, relays, or oracles is responsible for confirming cross-chain transactions. Users must trust that a majority (or some quorum) of these actors behave honestly, but these actors are often economically incentivized (e.g. via staked collateral or bonded slashing conditions) to uphold the bridge’s integrity. Examples include \textit{Axelar}~\cite{2021AxelarEcosystems}, \textit{Wormhole}~\cite{wormhole2024}, \textit{LayerZero}~\cite{layerzero2023}, \textit{Multichain}~\cite{multichain2024}, and Celer’s \textit{cBridge}~\cite{celer_cbridge}, all of which use a network of validators or signers to attest to events on one chain and authorize actions on another. Such bridges are sometimes called \textquote{permissioned} or federated bridges, since a predefined set of entities must sign off on transfers. Their trust model is stronger than a single custodian, but not as strong as native chain verification: users are effectively trusting that M-of-N validators will not collude maliciously. This model has become the norm for linking heterogeneous L1s that cannot run light clients of each other. For instance, when bridging assets from Ethereum to BNB Chain or Solana, often a coalition of validators (running a dedicated bridge blockchain or an off-chain oracle network) will verify the deposit on the source chain and then mint a wrapped asset on the destination chain. Such designs minimize trust relative to a single custodian, but they remain exposed to validator compromise or consensus failure (i.e., if the validator majority is subverted, the bridge can be exploited). 

At the far end of the spectrum are trusted bridges, which place full faith in a central authority or a fixed small multisig with no direct recourse if that trust is broken. These include custodial bridges (e.g., wrapped Bitcoin via BitGo’s WBTC, where a single custodian holds the asset reserve) and some early cross-chain bridges run by a single team or exchange. A notable example was Binance’s original bridge connecting Ethereum and BSC (BNB Chain), essentially operated by Binance as a trusted custodian. Similarly, the \textit{Ronin bridge} for Axie Infinity (prior to its 2022 hack) relied on just 9 validators controlled by a single organization (Sky Mavis) and its partners, effectively a federated multisig under one entity’s control. Such trusted models have minimal decentralization: users must trust that the custodian or signers will never misbehave, as there are no protocol-level penalties or verifications of their actions. While simple and fast to deploy, these bridges have the weakest security guarantees and have often proven fragile under attack, such as the \textit{Ronin bridge} hack of 2022 (see Table~\ref{tab:bridge_attacks}). 


\noindent\textbf{Connectivity Trends}: Table~\ref{tab:bridgetrustmatrix} shows the current cross-chain topology with clear patterns. Ecosystems with homogeneous technology tend to use trustless native bridges among themselves, whereas heterogeneous connections gravitate toward trust-minimized hubs. The Cosmos ecosystem is a prime example of the former: dozens of Cosmos-SDK chains (e.g., Osmosis, Cosmos Hub, Cronos and Juno) all interconnect via IBC channels with no external mediators~\cite{cosmos2023}, achieving a high degree of seamless, trustless interoperability. This strategy uses Cosmos’s uniform consensus framework. IBC works because each chain can run a light client of the other. The result is a richly connected \textquote{Interchain} where assets and data flow freely between sovereign zones, secured only by the zones’ own validator sets. However, a constraint of IBC (and any light-client bridge) is that both chains must support the protocol and often require fast finality. This limitation historically restricted Cosmos’s ability to connect to non-Cosmos chains, such as Ethereum, which did not natively support IBC. 

To bridge out to Ethereum and other ecosystems, Cosmos projects deployed separate bridge modules and networks. For example, the Gravity Bridge chain and Axelar network act as Cosmos-Ethereum bridges by having their validator sets observe events on Ethereum and vice versa. These are trust-minimized solutions (essentially multisig validator bridges) added onto Cosmos to import assets like USDC, DAI, and WETH into the IBC realm. Indeed, cross-chain usage data indicates heavy traffic along these routes: Ethereum-to-Osmosis is consistently a top-volume pair, with bridged USDC flowing into Cosmos DeFi apps as a primary stablecoin. Thus, Cosmos’s connectivity strategy combines trustless internal interoperability (IBC for Cosmos-to-Cosmos) with bridging hubs for external connectivity, introducing some trust assumptions to facilitate connections with Ethereum and beyond. Notably, the Cosmos community is actively working to extend IBC’s trustless model to other ecosystems. For instance, Composable Finance’s Picasso project has enabled IBC connections to Polkadot parachains and even to Ethereum via a hybrid IBC+zero-knowledge bridge. This suggests a future where Cosmos’s trustless interoperability may expand across ecosystems, thereby reducing the need for trusted intermediaries. 

Ethereum and its orbiting chains illustrate a different connectivity paradigm. As the largest DeFi L1, Ethereum is the common denominator for many bridging links. Virtually every major L1 (Solana, BNB Chain, Avalanche, Polygon, Fantom, etc.) has at least one bridge to Ethereum, given the high value of assets and liquidity on Ethereum. However, these Ethereum-centric bridges are typically not trustless; instead, they rely on third-party networks or federations to relay messages. For example, \textit{Wormhole} operates as a cross-chain message network connecting Ethereum with Solana, BSC, Avalanche, Polygon, and others, using a guardian consensus (a set of 19 nodes) to validate transfers. When a user moves ETH from Ethereum to Solana via \textit{Wormhole}, the ETH is locked in a \textit{Wormhole} contract on Ethereum, and a wrapped ETH is minted on Solana, based on a signed attestation by the guardian network. This design is trust-minimized (no single custodian, but users trust the majority of guardians). 

Other popular Ethereum bridges follow similar patterns: \textit{Multichain} (Anyswap) uses a rotated set of MPC signers to custody and mint assets between Ethereum and numerous EVM chains; Celer \textit{cBridge} uses a Proof-of-Stake validator set to oversee transfers. In all these cases, Ethereum-L1 bridges tend to introduce a separate middleware layer. An important special case is Ethereum’s own Layer-2 networks: bridging between Ethereum and its rollups is typically built into the protocol with robust security (e.g., users withdraw from Optimism/Arbitrum by presenting a proof on L1, without trusting an outside party). Those rollup bridges are essentially \textquote{trustless} (secured by Ethereum’s consensus and the rollup’s fraud/validity proofs) and thus differ from third-party bridges between independent L1s. 

When considering all these aspects, Ethereum’s connectivity strategy has a duality: internally (with rollups) it achieves near-trustless connectivity by design, whereas externally (with other L1s) it relies on a patchwork of trust-minimized bridge providers. The trend, however, is toward improving security; for example, research into Ethereum light-client bridges and the recent deployment of Chainlink’s CCIP indicate a push for more standardized, resilient cross-chain links. 

Outside the Ethereum/Cosmos spheres, other ecosystems have pursued their own bridging approaches with mixed strategies. Solana, for instance, being a high-throughput non-EVM chain, developed ties to Ethereum and others mainly through third-party bridge networks. \textit{Wormhole}’s origin was as the Solana-Ethereum bridge (also known as Portal), and it remains the primary connector between Solana and multiple other chains. Solana also saw bridges like Allbridge and Portal Token Bridge facilitating asset transfers to BNB Chain, Polygon, and Terra. These solutions have generally been trust-minimized (a set of signers or validators) rather than trustless, due to Solana’s distinct architecture, which makes light-client verification on Ethereum or vice versa non-trivial. 
One constraint Solana faced is that its very high throughput (50K tx/sec) and non-EVM-based execution environment meant fewer independent bridge options. The ecosystem largely relied on \textit{Wormhole}, which introduced a single point of failure for connectivity. Recent developments are changing this: projects are working on light-client bridges (e.g., using Solana's clients), and even \textit{Cosmos’s IBC} was adapted in 2023 to work from Solana via an adapter chain. Still, Solana’s cross-chain connectivity is primarily driven by multi-signature bridge protocols rather than native, trustless channels. The BNB Chain ecosystem likewise began with a highly centralized bridging model. BNB Chain actually comprises a dual-chain system, consisting of an application-focused Beacon Chain and an EVM-compatible Smart Chain, bridged by the \textquote{Token Hub}. The BSC Token Hub initially operated under the tight control of a few validators (effectively a trusted multisig), as evidenced by the fact that a successful attack in 2022 was able to forge just two messages and mint 2 million BNB (Table~\ref{tab:bridge_attacks}, an exploit that even forced a temporary halt to the chain.  In response, BNB Chain has moved to increase the security of its bridges (e.g., raising multisig thresholds and improving key management) and has also encouraged the use of external bridges. 

Nowadays, BNB Chain is connected to Ethereum and others via both Binance’s official bridge (still centrally governed) and alternatives like Celer, deBridge~\cite{debridge}, and LayerZero~\cite{layerzero}, which are more decentralized. Nevertheless, BNB’s connectivity strategy remains cautious: its native bridge prioritizes speed and user experience (at the cost of trust), whereas third-party solutions offer decentralization but introduce dependency on external validator sets. We observe a similar pattern in other ecosystems, such as Polygon (the Polygon PoS bridge utilizes a 5-of-8 multisig to checkpoint withdrawals, a somewhat trusted design, alongside newer trust-minimized options) and Tron (which relies on custodial bridges for assets like BTC/ETH via BitGo, or exchange-mediated transfers). In contrast, Polkadot takes a route closer to Cosmos: all parachains in a Polkadot parachain cluster are natively interoperable via the XCM (Cross-Consensus Message) format~\cite{xcm}, which is \textquote{trustless and secure} for in-network messaging. Polkadot’s relay chain ensures the validity of messages between parachains, so within this ecosystem, cross-chain actions (such as token transfers and remote contract calls) don’t require external trust. However, bridging Polkadot or Kusama (i.e., Polkadot's test network) to external chains still entails separate bridge projects (often with their own validator sets or light clients). Efforts are underway (e.g., Snowfork and Darwinia for Ethereum-Polkadot bridges) to make those as trust-minimized as possible, potentially even integrating light-client verification. 
\fi 

\section{Descriptions of \textdagger Attack Vectors}
\label{appsec:vectors}
In Table~\ref{tab:attack_vectors_categorized}, we used \textdagger~to indicate that these vulnerabilities only apply under certain design conditions. We now elaborate on those conditions.


\noindent\textbf{V1: Fake Burn/Lock Proofs.}
Tokens are locked or burned on the source chain, X, before minting can occur on the destination chain, Y. A cryptographic proof sent from X to Y is required for confirmation before minting can occur. This attack involves forging the proof somehow to trick the Y chain into minting before any proper burn or lock has actually happened.

\noindent\textbf{V2: Malicious Transaction Modification.}
Transaction details (addresses, number of tokens, gas fees, etc.) are sent from the source chain to the destination chain during a transaction. Malicious transaction modification involves somehow intercepting this detailed payload and asserting some fake payload. This can be done off-chain with protocols that include some centralized sequencer, or on-chain when the destination bridge is looking for the detailed payload.

\noindent\textbf{V3: Reentry Attack}
Occurs when some external function is called multiple times before the initial execution has completed. This attack vector was used in the famous DAO attack, where the token minting was executed many times before the balance was checked and updated.

\noindent\textbf{V4: Integer Overflow/Underflow}
When overflow or underflow remain unchecked, attackers can induce attacks by creating unexpected results. A wrap-around can also happen, where the maximum value can wrap around to the minimum value, or vice versa, creating room for exploitation.

\noindent\textbf{V5: Access Control Flaws}
 Access control is very important in maintaining control over a contract, and if unchecked, an attacker can impersonate a contract owner and manipulate the entire contract.

 \noindent\textbf{V6: Timestamp Manipulation}
 A large number of blockchain protocols require some timestamp with loose limits to allow for network delays. These loose limits allow attackers to falsify timestamps that will still be considered valid by the protocol, leading to vulnerable behavior in the protocol.

 \noindent\textbf{V7: Inconsistent Cross-Chain Transfers}
 Bridges can implement different methods for withdrawing and depositing, such as burning when performing a withdrawal but minting on a deposit. When these operations are different, attackers can take advantage of a lack of one-to-one consistency.

 \noindent\textbf{V8: Replay Attacks}
 Attackers can take a message in transit that was validated, then delay and retransmit the original message in order to assume validation but cause effects unintended by the original message.

 \noindent\textbf{V9: Oracle Manipulation}
 Oracles being used to provide data, such as token pricing, can be points of vulnerability if an attacker were to take control of the oracle and provide fake data, leading to incorrect fees or token amounts during minting or burning. Oracles can provide more data than just token prices, leading to more points of possible attack.

 \noindent\textbf{V10: Consensus Failure}
 Commonly referred to as a 51\% attack, if an attacker were to control 51\% of the mining or staking power (depending on the protocol) then that attacker would be able to create a new chain segment that builds faster than the legitimate chain. This control can lead to false transactions that could mint tokens on other chains.

\noindent\textbf{V11: Malicious Custodian Manipulation}
Custodial wallets are commonly used to hold locked tokens or private keys via a centralized source. Compromising or maliciously colluding with this custodial wallet leads to access of locked tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data.

\noindent\textbf{V12: Key Leakage / Private Key Theft}
Locked assets can exist within bridges accessible by private keys and bridges themselves can hold keys along with their controlling proxy contracts. Any compromising of these keys through phishing or other targeted attacks can create vulnerabilities, potentially giving an attacker full control over a bridge.

\noindent\textbf{V13: Race Condition Attacks}
A vulnerability is created when two or more processes access the same data, such as when withdrawals and deposits are processed without proper synchronization, and conflicting transactions can occur.

\noindent\textbf{V14: Unsafe External Call Exploits}
When a smart contract accesses some external call to third-party contracts or off-chain contracts, these new calls create vulnerability in their own code security that can be exploited.

\noindent\textbf{V15: Forged Account Attacks}
Occurs when an account or address is deliberately created to impersonate the identity of another party. This can lead to the redirection of tokens in a bridge structure if a fake account is validated.

\noindent\textbf{V16: Malicious Event Log Manipulation}
Key actions are logged by smart contracts, typically off-chain. Falsification of the event log can lead to relaying of some fake events that have not happened. This results in tricking the chain into a false state.

\noindent\textbf{V17: Denial of Service Attacks}
Bridge contracts can be flooded with transactions and requests sent by an attacker, overwhelming the bridge protocol and slowing processes for all transactions on the bridge. This could potentially lead to the complete halting of a bridge protocol.

\noindent\textbf{V18: Arithmetic Accuracy Deviation}
Exact calculations are required to maintain consistency across chains. Tiny errors in integer overflow or underflow can cause precision errors, leading to inconsistency between chain states.

