The set $\cA''$ has size at least $\frac{2}{3}|\cA''|\geq \frac{2}{3}\mu n$. Moreover, a curve $a\in \cA''$ has no endpoints in $S_i\cup S_{i+1}$, which is a Jordan region bounded by $b_{2i-1}, b_{2i+3}$, the subcurve of $a_1$ from $p(b_{2i-1})$ to $p(b_{2i+3})$ and the subcurve of $a_2$ from $q(b_{2i-1})$ to $q(b_{2i+3})$. Also, $a$ does not intersect the parts of the boundary of $S_i\cup S_{i+1}$ on $a_1$ and $a_2$. Let $x_1,\dots,x_s$ be the intersection points of $a$ with $b_{2i-1}\cup b_{2i+1}\cup b_{2i+3}$, where the indices follow the ordering of $a$. Then $x_1,x_s\in b_{2i-1}\cup b_{2i+3}$ and there is some $1<p<s$ such that $x_p\in b_{2i+1}$. Hence, there exists an index $1<q<s$ such that $x_q\in b_{2i+1}$ and $x_{q+1}\in b_{2i-1}\cup b_{2i+3}$. Without loss of generality, assume that $x_{q+1}\in b_{2i-1}$, the other case can be handled similarly. Then the subcurve of $a$ between $x_q$ and $x_{q+1}$ is contained in $S_i$, and it intersects every element of $\cB_{2i}$. Here, we are using that $a_1$ intersects the elements of $\cB_{2i}$ positively, and thus the curves of $\cB_{2i}$ pass through $S_i$, joining $a_1$ and $a_2$.

In conclusion, there is a set $\cA'''\subset \cA''$ of size at least $|\cA''|/2\geq \mu n/3$ such that that either every element of $\cA'''$ intersects every element of $\cB_{2i}$, or every element of $\cA'''$ intersects every element of $\cB_{2i+2}$. This finishes the proof.
