mpetency questions. For \textbf{Scheduling \& Queue Analysis} (C5.1 - C5.2), the \texttt{Job} class is crucial for questions about job submission and waiting times, while \texttt{JobMetric} provides data on the time a job spends in the queue. These classes, along with the property \texttt{hasJobMetric}, are sufficient for addressing the questions related to job queue analysis. Finally, in \textbf{Cross-System \& Comparative Analytics} (C6.1 - C6.5), the \texttt{HPCSystem} class is vital for comparing different systems, while \texttt{JobMetric} enables comparison of metrics such as job execution time and energy efficiency. The \texttt{Job} class provides comparative metrics for job executions across multiple systems. Together, these classes, with their respective properties, allow for a detailed comparison of systems and performance across different contexts.

