% \end{equation}

% \noindent where $\mu(\xi)$ is the step function defined as $\mu=1$ if $\xi \ge 0$ and $0$ otherwise, and $\Delta^{\text{max,r}}_i$ is the measure \eqref{eq:max} applied to the null model. The values of \eqref{eq:R2} for the three years are $50\%$, $34\%$ and $45\%$, values very similar to what observed for \eqref{eq:R1} which further confirms what was observed from the analysis of $g_i$, $\Delta_i^{\text{av}}$ and $\Delta_i^{\text{max}}$ previously. 

The analysis of the confidence intervals shows another behavior: Sardex users' neighbors are more similar to each other than in randomized connections. Let us define the confidence interval of the $i$-th user as 
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_i^{\text{conf}} = \max_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} |\mathcal{N}_j|-\min_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} |\mathcal{N}_j|, \label{eq:ci}
\end{equation}
\noindent which represents  the maximum number of neighbors minus the minimum number of neighbors among  the neighbors of the $i$-th user. Smaller values of $\Delta_i^{\text{conf}}$ suggest stronger alignments, indicating that the neighbors of the $i$-th user are more homogeneous. The boxplots of~\eqref{eq:ci} reported in Figure~\ref{fig:intConf} show that, compared to the null model, a larger portion of users have relatively small confidence intervals, suggesting some homogeneity among the degrees of proximity of Sardex users. 
%This conclusion is confirmed by the  rate $\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \mu (x_i \geq \alpha_i) \mu (x_i \leq \beta_i)$ which quantifies the fraction of individuals whose out-degree falls within the confidence interval of their neighbors. The values of \eqref{eq:R3} for the three years are $22\%$, $15\%$ and $20\%$, confirming that only a moderate fraction of users have an out-degree between the minimum and maximum of their neighbors, in line with the distribution of the quantity $\Delta_i^{\text{out}}$.

