\begin{abstract}
Despite the remarkable progress of large language models (LLMs) across various domains, their capacity to predict retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) risk remains largely unexplored. To address this gap, we introduce a novel Chinese benchmark dataset, termed CROP, comprising 993 admission records annotated with low, medium, and high-risk labels. To systematically examine the predictive capabilities and affective biases of LLMs in ROP risk stratification, we propose Affective-ROPTester, an automated evaluation framework incorporating three prompting strategies: Instruction-based, Chain-of-Thought (CoT), and In-Context Learning (ICL). The Instruction scheme assesses LLMs’ intrinsic knowledge and associated biases, whereas the CoT and ICL schemes leverage external medical knowledge to enhance predictive accuracy. Crucially, we integrate emotional elements at the prompt level to investigate how different affective framings influence the model's ability to predict ROP and its bias patterns. Empirical results derived from the CROP dataset yield two principal observations. First, LLMs demonstrate limited efficacy in ROP risk prediction when operating solely on intrinsic knowledge, yet exhibit marked performance gains when augmented with structured external inputs. Second, affective biases are evident in the model outputs, with a consistent inclination toward overestimating medium- and high-risk cases. Third, compared to negative emotions, positive emotional framing contributes to mitigating predictive bias in model outputs. These findings highlight the critical role of affect-sensitive prompt engineering in enhancing diagnostic reliability and emphasize the utility of Affective-ROPTester as a framework for evaluating and mitigating affective bias in clinical language modeling systems. We aspire for our proposal to deeply explore the capabilities of LLMs for ROP prediction and contribute to the advancement of the healthcare community.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Retinopathy of prematurity, Large language models, Affective, Chain-of-Thought, In-Context Learning.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{I}{n} recent years, supported by powerful computing resources, large language models (LLMs)~\cite{meta2025llama,guo2025deepseek} have demonstrated impressive capabilities. Their widespread applicability has not only transformed traditional NLP tasks such as summarization~\cite{zhao2023softmax} and mathematical reasoning, but also enabled them to achieve state-of-the-art performance in domains including education~\cite{jia2025uni,jia2025towards,wu2025query}, finance~\cite{wu2023bloomberggpt}, and healthcare~\cite{lin2025cross,he2025survey,wu2025harnessing}.
Smart healthcare systems, powered by LLMs and utilizing patient medical notes, aim to predict the most likely diseases and assist doctors in making accurate clinical decisions.
Despite several studies confirm the potential advantages of LLMs in advancing smart healthcare~\cite{tian2023chimed,wu2023megacare}, research on predicting the risk of retinopathy of prematurity (ROP)~\cite{postaci2024ability} remains underexplored.

ROP, a retinal vascular disease occurring in preterm and low weight infants, has a pathogenesis that remains unclear \cite{chiang2021international,Qiaoyu2022ROP}.
Traditionally, the diagnosis of ROP has depended heavily on medical records and imaging examinations \cite{lin2024oxygenation}.
Despite the fact that clinical practices are capable of diagnosing retinopathy in premature infants, the early prediction of those at risk for such conditions is critical for effective intervention, representing an ongoing challenge in the field~\cite{wu2022development}.
With research indicating that known risk factors for ROP include birth weight, gestational age, multiple gestation, mode of delivery, duration of oxygen therapy, and oxygen concentration, among others~\cite{Qiaoyu2022ROP}.
Considering the powerful reasoning capabilities and extensive medical knowledge of LLMs, this paper proactively leverages LLMs to predict ROP risk using only medical notes. 
Hence, three analogy questions emerge: {(1) {\bf How effective are LLMs at predicting ROP risk?\label{ques:effectiveness}} (2) {\bf Do LLMs exhibit inherent bias in their predictions of ROP risk?}\label{ques:bias} (3) {\bf To what extent do affective elements embedded at the prompt level influence the predictive outcomes and bias of LLMs?
}\label{ques:affective}}

%Due to previous research focusing on the diagnosis of ROP, which has resulted in a lack of benchmark datasets related to the prediction of ROP risk.
Previous research has primarily focused on the diagnosis of ROP based on medical records and imaging examinations, resulting in a lack of benchmark datasets for predicting ROP risk using only medical notes.
To address these challenges, we construct a Chinese ROP risk prediction dataset named CROP, which includes only the initial hospital admission records of preterm infants.
The dataset comprises 993 available admission records of preterm infants, which are evenly divided into low, medium, and high-risk categories.
Our motivation is to leverage LLMs to predict ROP risk using only the admission records of the infants.

