To validate the capabilities and potential biases of LLMs in predicting ROP risk, we propose the Affective-ROPTester, which includes the Instruction scheme, the Chain of Thought (CoT) scheme, and the In-context Learning (ICL) scheme, as shown in Figure \ref{fig1}.
The Instruction scheme validates whether relying solely on the knowledge of LLMs can effectively predict ROP risk and explores whether the model exhibits any biases.
For the CoT and ICL schemes, we introduce known risk factors and demonstration examples as external knowledge to further validate the performance of LLMs in predicting ROP risk.
Crucially, we incorporate emotional elements at the prompt level to investigate how different affective framings impact the ability of LLMs to predict ROP and reveal potential biases.
We conduct comprehensive experiments to assess the effectiveness of multiple state-of-the-art LLMs in predicting ROP risk.
The experimental results indicate that relying solely on the internal knowledge of the models is insufficient for effectively predicting ROP risk, and that LLMs such as Qwen-2.5, GPT-4o, etc., exhibit significant biases.
Moreover, positive emotional framing helps improve predictive accuracy and mitigate bias.
Ultimately, the introduction of external knowledge effectively improved the accuracy of ROP risk prediction. For instance, Qwen-2.5 model achieves an accuracy of 61.33\% in the CoT scheme.
The main contributions of this paper are summarized in the following outline:
\begin{itemize}
    \item To bridge the dataset gap between LLMs and ROP risk prediction, this paper constructs a new ROP risk prediction benchmark dataset. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first Chinese dataset for predicting ROP risk using only the admission records of infants.
    \item In this paper, we propose the Affective-ROPTester, which includes the Instruction, CoT, and ICL schemes. Notably, different affective framings are integrated within the Instruction scheme to examine the impact of emotional cues. These schemes thoroughly validate the capabilities of LLMs in predicting ROP risk, both intrinsically and with the introduction of external knowledge, and could serve as benchmarks for ROP risk prediction research.
    And also give an instrcution for researchers to design fine-grained affective prompts for LLMs to liberate the potential ability and let more doctors benefit from Affective-ROPTester.
    %\item In this paper, we propose the Affective-ROPTester, which includes the Instruction, CoT, and ICL schemes within different affective representations in prompt level. These schemes thoroughly validate the capabilities of LLMs in predicting ROP risk, both intrinsically and with the introduction of external knowledge, and could serve as benchmarks for ROP risk prediction research. And also give an instrcution for researchers to design fine-grained affective prompts for LLMs to liberate the potential ability and let more doctors benefit from Affective-ROPTester.
    %\yh{
    %\item In this paper, we present ROPTester, a comprehensive evaluation framework comprising three prompting strategies—Instruction, CoT, and ICL—each integrated with distinct affective representations at the prompt level. These schemes collectively assess the predictive performance of LLMs in ROP risk stratification, both through intrinsic model knowledge and via the incorporation of external clinical data. }

    \item We demonstrate the potential biases of LLMs in predicting ROP risk, as experiments reveal a tendency for the models to output medium and high risk levels. Moreover, this bias can be mitigated through the introduction of external knowledge, and the integration of positive affective elements into the prompt serves a similar role.

    \item Beyond serving as a benchmark for future research on LLM-based medical risk prediction, Affective-ROPTester provides actionable guidance for constructing fine-grained, affect-aware prompts. This facilitates the full exploitation of LLM capabilities and promotes broader adoption among clinicians seeking interpretable and emotionally balanced decision support tools.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\noindent{\bf LLMs for Ophthalmology:}
With LLMs develop~\cite{xiao2025exploring,jia2025seeing}, their applications in the medical domain are increasingly widespread~\cite{liu2024time,lin2024has,han2024chain}, especially in ophthalmology.
Postaci et al.~\cite{postaci2024ability} evaluate the capability of LLMs to generate materials for ROP, which includes readability, accuracy, and comprehensiveness.
Holmes et al.~\cite{holmes2023evaluating} evaluate the performance of LLMs in the pediatric ophthalmology consultation, demonstrating that LLMs have the capability to assist in ophthalmic diagnosis.
Engin et al.~\cite{engin2024exploring} hypothesize LLMs as virtual consultants and test their effectiveness in ROP consultations.
Ermis et al.~\cite{ermis2024assessing} study the response capability of LLMs to common questions in ROP, with evaluation metrics including readability and appropriateness.
Shi et al.~\cite{shi2023ffa} develop an interactive system that harnesses LLMs for report generation and visual question answering in the context of fundus fluorescein angiography.
Tan et al.~\cite{tan2024fine} fine-tune LLMs to evaluate the accuracy of responses generated by LLM-based chatbots to ophthalmology-related patient inquiries.
To enhance the accuracy of ophthalmology-related question-answering, Nguyen et al.~\cite{nguyen2024advancing} leverage retrieval-augmented generation algorithms to optimize model performance.

\noindent{\bf ROP Diagnosis and Prediction:}
Traditional ROP research primarily focuses on diagnosing whether premature infants suffer from retinal pathologies, utilizing medical records and imaging examinations~\cite{agrawal2022deep}.
For instance, Kumar et al.~\cite{kumar2023deep} propose a deep convolutional neural network for the automatic detection of retinopathy in premature infants.
Sankari et al.~\cite{sankari2023automated} develop a hybrid deep learning network for the detection of ROP, which exhibits feasible accuracy.
Redd et al.~\cite{redd2019evaluation} leverage fundus examination to assess the severity of retinal pathologies in premature infants based on the deep learning model.
Unlike diagnosis, predicting the risk of ROP is more challenging. 
Wu et al.~\cite{wu2022development} design the OC-Net and SE-Net models to predict ROP risk, with retinal images as inputs to the models.
Unlike previous work, in this paper, we systematically assess the ability of LLMs to predict ROP risk and the potential biases within the models, which use only the initial hospital admission records of preterm infants.

\noindent{\bf Affective bias:}
Affective bias is pervasive across large language models (LLMs), and a growing body of studies has shown that both its magnitude and its manifestations vary from one model to another~\cite{xiao2024atlantis,xiao2024vanessa}. Lin et al.~\cite{lin2024emo} investigate gender bias in speech emotion recognition models, revealing that female speakers generally achieve slightly better performance than male speakers and that typical self-supervised learning approaches exhibit notable gender bias. Kadan et al.~\cite{kadan2024understanding} examine latent affective biases in language models, showing that these models often incorrectly associate specific emotions with gender, race, or religion. 
To mitigate the model's bias toward emotion categories, Kang et al.~\cite{kang2020active} propose an active learning method for efficiently guiding human annotations to construct a less-biased and high-quality multi-label emotion corpus.
Mao et al.~\cite{mao2022biases} conduct a systematic empirical study on prompt-based emotion classification and affective recognition tasks, uncovering various biases in pretrained language models, such as biases related to the number of label categories.
In this paper, we explore whether leveraging affective prompts can enhance the model's ability to predict ROP risk and mitigate potential biases.

\section{Preliminary}\label{dataset}
\subsection{Task Formulation}%bias
We formulate the task of predicting ROP risk as a multi-class classification problem, with three categories: low-risk, medium-risk, and high-risk. 
Although the pathogenesis of ROP remains unclear, known risk factors for preterm infants include birth weight, gestational age, multiple gestation and mode of delivery, duration of oxygen therapy and oxygen concentration, red blood cell transfusion, and maternal conditions such as gestational hypertension and diabetes~\cite{Qiaoyu2022ROP}.
Therefore, the motivation for this task is to verify whether LLMs have the capability to predict the risk of retinopathy in premature infants based solely on admission notes. 
Concurrently, this study also aims to explore potential biases in existing LLMs that may affect their performance in predicting ROP risk.
Specifically, for a given electronic admission notes $x$ and a set of potential risk categories $\mathcal{Y}$. The task of predicting ROP risk leverages the reasoning capabilities of LLMs $\mathcal{M}$ to output the most probable lesion level from the potential risk set as its prediction. Consequently, the predicted label $y_{pre}$ is ascertained to have the maximal likelihood: 
{\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{5pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{5pt}
\begin{equation} 
y_{\text{pre}} = \underset{y_{j} \in \mathcal{Y}}{\mathrm{argmax}}\ p_{\mathcal{M}}(y_j | x),
\label{eq1}
\end{equation}}
\noindent where $y_j$ represents the true label of the $x$, and $\mathcal{Y}$ includes three categories: low, medium, and high of risk.

\subsection{Dataset Construction}
In contrast to previous datasets that include medical records and imaging examinations, we introduce the CROP dataset, a Chinese benchmark dataset dedicated to text-based ROP risk prediction research. 
Specifically, we collected the medical records of preterm infants who were diagnosed with groups of no ROP, mild ROP, and severe ROP. 
Mild ROP corresponds to type 2 ROP, while severe ROP includes type 1 ROP, aggressive ROP, and stages 4 and 5 ROP~\cite{fierson2018screening,wu2022development}. 
In all the medical records, the admission notes were extracted specifically for the task of predicting ROP risk. 
Simultaneously, based on the final diagnosis, we labeled the extracted admission notes as low-risk, medium-risk, and high-risk. 

To construct the dataset for predicting ROP, we collect 3,922 original records, of which only 331 notes are high-risk samples.
Therefore, to maintain sample balance, we establish that the CROP benchmark dataset contains 993 notes from preterm infant admissions, among which the low-risk, medium-risk, and high-risk categories of ROP each include 331 notes.
Although the purpose of this study is to verify whether the LLM itself has the capability to predict ROP risk, we also fine-tuned the LLM for comparison. Therefore, the test set in the CROP dataset includes only 331 notes, with the details presented in Table \ref{tab:dataset}.
Ethics Committee approval was obtained from Shenzhen Eye Hospital (NO. 2022KYPJ064) and the research was adhered to the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. All data were provided by Guangdong Women and Children Hospital. 
