\subsection{\!ROP Risk Prediction Capabilities of LLMs}
\noindent{\bf Performance of Instructional Scheme:}
%
First, we verified whether leveraging only the Instruction scheme enables LLMs to predict ROP risk? 
The experimental results, as shown in Table \ref{tab:main_results}, indicate that the majority of LLMs struggle to predict ROP risk, resulting in lower accuracy.
For instance, the accuracy of the LLaMA-3.3 model is 35.64\%, which suggests that relying solely on the knowledge of LLMs is ineffective for predicting ROP risk and providing effective diagnostic assistance.
Furthermore, as the number of model parameters increases, the DeepSeek-R1 model achieves the best performance in the open-source general models setting. Additionally, compared to the LLaMA-3.3 model, the distilled DeepSeek-R1 model shows a significant improvement, with an increase in accuracy of 9.97\%.

\noindent{\bf Performance of CoT Scheme:}
We verify the performance of the CoT scheme on LLMs.
It is evident that, upon introducing the CoT, the prediction accuracy of the LLMs significantly improved compared to the Instructional scheme.
For example, the LLaMA-3.3 shows an average improvement of 25.21\%.
Simultaneously, the Qwen-2.5 and Baichuan-4 models achieve the best performance in the open source and closed source model settings, respectively.
The results above indicate that the CoT scheme helps to enhance model predictive performance and promotes the alignment of reasoning capabilities with known risk factors in ROP.

