\subsection{ROP Risk Prediction with Different Affective Framings}
\noindent{\bf Performance Comparison of Different Affective Framings}:
As shown in Table \ref{tab:affective}, different affective framings have a significant impact on the model's ability to predict ROP risk. We observe that, compared to neutral and positive affective framings, negative affective framing has a notably adverse effect on prediction accuracy. For example, in the DeepSeek-R1 model, the accuracy under negative affective framing is 40.18\%, while under neutral affective framing it rises to 43.50\%, showing an improvement of 3.32\%. Furthermore, the accuracy under positive affective framing reaches 49.24\%, representing a further increase of 9.06\%. This pattern is also observed across other models. Therefore, we conclude that, compared to negative affective framing, neutral or positive affective framing contributes to improved accuracy in ROP risk prediction, addressing Question \hyperref[ques:affective]{3}.

\noindent{\bf Impact of Different Affective Framings on Bias}:
In Section \ref{bias_llm}, we observed that LLMs tend to predict medium and high risk for ROP, which may be attributed to the model's overly pessimistic assumptions about premature infants. A plausible solution is to incorporate affective prompts to mitigate biased predictions. As shown in Figure \ref{fig_affective}, we present the model's risk prediction tendencies under different affective framings. It is evident that under negative and neutral affective framings, the model tends to predict medium and high risk, which is consistent with the observations in Section \ref{bias_llm}.
In contrast, when exposed to different affective framings, this bias is significantly mitigated. For example, when the input sample corresponds to a low-risk case, the probability of the model predicting high risk is 76.79\% under the negative affective framing, 50\% under the neutral affective framing, and drops to only 21.43\% under the positive affective framing. This indicates that, in the context of ROP risk prediction, prompts infused with positive affective framing help reduce the bias exhibited by LLMs.

\subsection{Ablation Experiments of Affective-ROPTester}
\noindent{\bf Different Risk Factors for ROP: }To analyze the impact of known risk factors on ROP prediction in the CoT scheme, we conduct ablation experiments.
From Table \ref{tab:factor}, we find that when using only gestational age and birth weight for the CoT, the optimal ROP risk prediction occurs in the LLaMA-3.3 and GPT-4o models.
For example, the average metric is 63.03\% in the GPT-4o model, which is higher than the results of other risk factors.
The reason for this phenomenon lies in the fact that, compared to other risk factors, gestational age and birth weight possess well-defined numerical foundations, facilitating more effective assistance in model predictions.
