To further verify the ability of LLMs with different parameter sizes to predict ROP risk, we conduct experiments using the Qwen-2.5 series of models, which range from 0.5B to 72B parameters.
As shown in Table \ref{tab:size}, we observe that in the Instructional scheme, the accuracy of model predictions for ROP risk is not positively correlated with the size of the model parameters.
This is because relying solely on the intrinsic knowledge of the model is insufficient for an accurate diagnosis of ROP risk.
In the CoT and ICL schemes, the incorporation of external knowledge significantly enhances the accuracy of ROP risk predictions. 
Furthermore, with an increase in the model's parameter size, there is a corresponding enhancement in its reasoning capabilities, which in turn progressively elevates the accuracy of ROP risk predictions. 

