\noindent{\bf Results of the Unbalanced Dataset:}\label{c.7}
To further demonstrate the reliability of our findings, such as the potential bias in the model's prediction of ROP, we conduct experiments with all the samples (totaling 3,922) without considering the balance of sample categories.
The unbalanced dataset includes 2,813 low-risk samples, 848 medium-risk samples, and 331 high-risk samples.
The experimental results are shown in Table \ref{tab:unblanced}. We found that the accuracy of the model's predictions is lower compared to the results in Table \ref{tab:main_results}. For example, the accuracy of the Qwen-2.5 model is only 19.01\% in the Instruction scheme.
The reason is that the model has a bias towards predicting ROP as high-risk or medium-risk, while the unbalanced dataset contains a larger number of low-risk samples, which leads to a higher proportion of incorrect predictions.
The results mentioned above further validate our findings that the model has a bias toward predicting ROP.



\noindent{\bf Disease Prediction vs. Disease Diagnosis:} \label{c.6}
In the domain of traditional smart healthcare, LLMs may be employed to diagnose whether patients have diseases based on medical records and imaging examinations. This approach is utilized to investigate the current health status of patients.
For example, in the case of ROP, LLMs are capable of diagnosing whether premature infants have developed retinal pathology~\cite{sankari2023automated}.
However, our research differs from traditional ROP diagnosis. Instead, it focuses on predicting the risk of ROP in premature infants, based solely on admission notes.
Our motivation lies in predicting the risk of ROP in premature infants in advance, under the premise of an unclear pathogenesis of ROP, with the goal of early detection and treatment.

