In this section we are going to develop a tool called $\ReFLECT$, a modification of $\LECT$ formatted to establish a distance on filtered complexes. 
The motivation behind this tool is primarily theoretical, although it has interesting noise reduction properties. 

It is the opinion of the authors that there is an unfortunate element of arbitrariness within the restriction of the filtration values to the positive real numbers for the $\SELECT$ distance defined in the previous section.
This comes from the fact that the range between the minimum filtration value and 0 is integrated over but does not carry with it any change in the Euler Characteristic. 
To illustrate the problem, consider two filtrations $\phi, \phi'$ of the same complex $K$, such that the minimum value of $\phi$ was 5, and for every $\sigma \in K$, $\phi'(\sigma) = \phi(\sigma) - 3$. 
The $\SELECT$ distance of the same two embeddings $f,g$ of $K$ would be less under $\phi'$ even though the embeddings and complex are the same, only because the minimum value of $\phi'$ is closer to 0, and so the $\chi(K)$ would be integrated over for a shorter interval. 

The nicest theoretical region of integration would be over the real numbers, however with $\SELECT$ this is not possible because we lack the compact support we need to make the integral finite. 
The second natural choice of region of integration would be the closed interval of the minimum and maximum filtration values. 
However, crucially, with $\SELECT$ distance, at the minimum value the ECT distance of the entire complex is only present in the integral in a measure zero interval, meaning our distance would not be able to distinguish between two complexes that were identical except for simplicies at the maximum filtration value. 

Our new tool $\ReFLECT$ gives us the ideal range of integration. 
It will also serve as a way of computing distances between complexes that are a specific kind of collapse of another, which we will define shortly. 

