    Let ${\mirror{f}_i}_{field}$ denote the field defined by $\mirror{f}_i(K_i)$, and $\hat{f}_i := \ReFLECT(K_i, \phi_i, f_i)$. 
    We define the \textbf{$\ReFLECT$ distance} between two embedded filtered complexes to be 
    $$d((K_1,\phi_1,f_1), (K_2,\phi_2,f_2))_p = \Big(\int_{\R } \int_{\S ^{d-1}}  \int_{\R } \bigm\vert \hat{f_1}(\nu,a,t)-\hat{f_2}(\nu,a,t) \bigm\vert^p  \ da \ d\nu \ dt\Big)^{1/p}.$$



\end{defn}
This distance is well defined for filtered embedded complexes %
that have level sets with equal Euler characteristic. 
It is a metric only up to subcomplexes of Euler characteristic 0 that live all within the same filtration value (due to the entire complex only being ``seen" by the integral for a subset of measure 0). 
\begin{rem}
We can disregard  condition (1) if we assume that the simplicial complexes live within a bounding ball with radius $B$, and use the ECT distance integrated over $[-B,B]$ for each level set.

    If we are disregarding the restriction on the Euler Characteristics being the same across all level sets, and considering the ECT distance within some bounding ball, this is a metric up to subcomplexes of \emph{any} Euler Characteristic all within the same filtration value. 
\end{rem}
The second restriction is simply to prevent any measure zero integration troubles in the $\;t$ direction
The first restriction of $\forall \;t \in \R , \ \chi(\phi_1 \inv (t) )  = \chi(\phi_2 \inv(t) ) $ may seem quite restrictive, but it applies to several important classes of simplicial complex, including different embeddings of the same abstract complex. 

In particular, this holds for deformation retracts (in the geometric setting, collapses in the abstract setting) along a particular filtration value. 
\begin{defn}
    Let $K = [v_0, v_1, \cdots v_k]$ be a filtered simplex with filtration function $\phi$. 
    If $\phi(v_i) = \phi(v_j), \ i\neq j$  for some $0\leq i,j, \leq k$, and $K' = [v_0,\cdots \hat{v_i},\cdots v_k]$ is a simplex such that $\chi(K) = \chi(K')$, we call $K'$ a \textbf{filtration-wise retract} of $K$. 
\end{defn}
Because homology does not change with deformation retracts (Theorem 1.3~\cite{dey2022computational}), the level sets retain their Euler Characteristic, thus making them comparable with this distance. 
One can also filtration-wise retract as much as possible, leaving one with a "skeleton" of the data, which we can find the distance of from the original data. 
Finding algorithms to efficiently compute deformation retracts is non trivial.~\cite{pritam2020collapses}
