\subsection{Effect of solver errors in sparse grid stochastic collocation}\label{sec:noisesc}
It is generally not possible to evaluate $q(\y)$ exactly: each evaluation used to build \eqref{eq:combinationtechnique} is an approximation $q^{\vMiModel}(\vCollocPt) \approx q(\vCollocPt)$
% \begin{equation}
%     q^{\vMiModel}(\vCollocPt) \approx q(\vCollocPt),
% \end{equation}
via a PDE solve where $\vMiModel$ \lt{is a multi-index of hyperparameters that control} the approximation fidelity (e.g.\ mesh size, solver tolerances,...).
\bk{Therefore,} a computable single-fidelity \ah{surrogate of the quantity of interest} can be formed using the combination technique \eqref{eq:combinationtechnique} with multi-index set $\miset$,
\begin{equation} \label{eq:approxcomputable}
    q(\y) \approx q^{\vMiModel,\miset}(\y) := \sum_{\vMi \in \miset} c_{\vMi} \interpOp{\vMi}[q^{\vMiModel}](\y).
\end{equation}
\bk{constructed using pointwise evaluations $q^{\vMiModel}(\vCollocPt)\approx q(\vCollocPt) \forall \vCollocPt \in \collocSet{\miset}$.}
% which is constructed via the pointwise approximations $q^{\vMiModel}(\vCollocPt)$ for each $\vCollocPt$ in the grid $\collocSet{\miset}$.
Again, as in \eqref{eq:truncated}, a truncated spectral expansion can be \mg{obtained} via a change of basis,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fullexpansion}
    q^{\vMiModel,\miset}(\y) \equiv \sum_{\vMiPoly\in\misetPoly(\miset)} \FT{q}^{\vMiModel,\miset}_{\vMiPoly} \chebBasis_{\vMiPoly}(\y).
\end{equation}
% As stated in \Cref{sec:introduction}, this paper is concerned with solver error corrupting the approximation.
% This effect is identified in the spectral expansion.
Each computable coefficient $\FT{q}^{\vMiModel,\miset}_{\vMiPoly}$ for $\vMiPoly\in\misetPoly(\miset)$ is an approximation \mg{of} the corresponding coefficient $\FT{q}_{\vMiPoly}$ from the \bk{full spectral expansion} \eqref{eq:spectral} and can be expressed as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:spectral-split}
    \FT{q}^{\vMiModel,\miset}_{\vMiPoly} = \underbrace{\FT{q}_{\vMiPoly}}_{(I)} + \underbrace{\FT{q}^{\miset}_{\vMiPoly} - \FT{q}_{\vMiPoly}}_{(II)} + \underbrace{\FT{q}^{\vMiModel,\miset}_{\vMiPoly} - \FT{q}^{\miset}_{\vMiPoly}}_{(III)}.
\end{equation}
The term $(I)$ in \eqref{eq:spectral-split} is the true spectral series coefficient.
For an analytic function $q(\y)$ in a spectral-type polynomial expansion, we expect the expansion coefficients $\{\FT{q}_{\vMiPoly}\}_{\N_0^{\nY}}$ to obey an a priori bound of the form 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:coeffdecay}
    % \lvert \FT{q}_{\vMiPoly} \rvert \leq C(q,\vMiPoly) \prod_{\iY=1}^{\nY} \ellipseR_{\iY}^{-\miPoly_{\iY}}
    \lvert \FT{q}_{\vMiPoly} \rvert \leq C(q,\vMiPoly) \prod_{\iY=1}^{\nY} e^{-g_{\iY}\miPoly_{\iY}}
\end{equation}
where $g_{\iY} > 0$ for all $\iY=1,2,...,\nY$ and $C(q,\vMiPoly)$ grows slowly as the polynomial degree $\vMiPoly$ increases.
For further details on analyticity and \bk{spectral polynomial expansion coefficient bounds} see, e.g.\, \cite{Nobile2008b}\cite[Chapter 3, Theorem 3.2]{Adcock2022}.
When the term $(I)$ dominates \eqref{eq:spectral-split} the coefficient is well approximated.
% % and can be bound a priori under \Cref{assumption:analytic}.
% \begin{lemma}[Spectral coefficient a priori bound]\label{theorem:coeffdecay}
%     For $q:\Gamma\to\R$ satisfying \Cref{assumption:analytic} the multi-variate Chebyshev coefficients of \eqref{eq:spectral} are bound a priori by
%     \begin{equation} \label{eq:coeffdecay}
%         % \begin{cases}
%             \lvert \FT{q}_{\vMiPoly} \rvert \leq \Vert \complex{q} \Vert_{L^\infty(\polyellipse_{\ellipseR};\C)} 2^{\Vert \vMiPoly \Vert_0} \prod_{\iY=1}^{\nY}  \ellipseR_{\iY}^{-\miPoly_{\iY}}.% , & \text{Chebyshev}, \\
%             % \lvert \FT{u}_{\vMiPoly} \rvert \leq \Vert \complex{u} \Vert_{L^\infty(\polyellipse_{\ellipseR})} C(\vMiPoly) \prod_{\iY=1}^{\nY} \ellipseR_{\iY}^{-\miPoly_{\iY}},                & \text{Legendre},  \\
%         % \end{cases}\label{eq:coeffdecay}
%     \end{equation}
%     where ${\Vert \vMiPoly \Vert_0}$ is the number of non-zero entries in the multi-index $\vMiPoly$ and $\Vert \complex{q} \Vert_{L^\infty(\polyellipse_{\ellipseR};\C)}:=\sup_{z\in\polyellipse_{\ellipseR}} \lvert \complex{q}(z) \rvert$.
%     %where $C(\vMiPoly):=\prod_{\substack{\iY \in 1,2,...,\nY \\{\miPoly_{\iY}\neq 0}}} \min\{2\rho_{\iY}, \frac{\pi}{2}(\rho_{\iY} + \rho_{\iY}^{-1})\} \sqrt{2 \miPoly_\iY + 1}$.
% \end{lemma}
% \begin{proof} See, for example, \cite[Theorem 3.2]{Adcock2022}.
% \end{proof}
% When $(I)$ is the dominant term in the splitting \eqref{eq:spectral-split} the approximation is accurate and the coefficients are expected to obey the a priori bound \eqref{eq:coeffdecay}.
% The truncation and solver errors mean this is not always the case.

The term $(II)$ represents the truncation error in the approximation.
This contains aliasing type effects in the truncated expansion.
This term is at its largest for the highest polynomial degree terms in the truncated approximation.
The effect of $(II)$ can be mitigated for a given polynomial degree $\vMiPoly$ by increasing the size of the polynomial approximation space.
Truncation effects are generally not a source of concern in the context of this work.

The term $(III)$ represents the solver error.
% Using the linearity of \eqref{eq:combinationtechnique} implies 
% ${q}^{\vMiModel,\miset} - {q}^{\miset}\equiv \interpOp{\miset}[q^{\vMiModel}-q]$
Linearity of the change of basis to the spectral polynomials and linearity of the combination technique construction \eqref{eq:combinationtechnique} implies $(III)$ is in fact the spectral coefficient of an interpolant of the solver error,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:interpoferror}
\sum_{\vMiPoly\in\misetPoly(\miset)} \left(\FT{q}^{\miModel,\miset}_{\vMiPoly}- \FT{q}^{\miset}_{\vMiPoly}\right)  \chebBasis_{\vMiPoly}(\y) \equiv \sum_{\vMi \in \miset} c_{\vMi} \interpOp{\vMi}[\ah{q^{\alpha} - q}](\y).
\end{equation}
If the solver error is constant over $\Gamma$, i.e.\ \mg{$[q^{\vMiModel,\miset} - q^{\miset}](\y) = C^{\vMiModel}\in\R$}, \bk{then the interpolant on the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:interpoferror} is exact and a constant}.
Therefore the term $(III)$ is a constant, and the only non-zero coefficient is for the constant polynomial (i.e.\ polynomial degree $\vMiPoly=\mistyle{0}$).
% If the model error is a Gaussian random variable the coefficients are also samples of a Gaussian random variable.
\bk{If instead} the solver error is more complex and appears highly oscillatory, possibly even seemingly random over $\Gamma$, many coefficients $\FT{q}^{\miModel,\miset}_{\vMiPoly}- \FT{q}^{\miset}_{\vMiPoly}$ will be non-zero and potentially large\bk{; this can be due to unresolved or preasymptotic meshes, coarse tolerances in iterative solvers, timestepping etc.}
It is this term $(III)$ that can corrupt the computable surrogate ${q}^{\vMiModel,\miset}$ in \eqref{eq:approxcomputable}.
% This can result in a \emph{spectral plateau}, observed due to all corrupted terms in the polynomial expansion \eqref{eq:fullexpansion} being of similar erroneous magnitude.
This can result in a \emph{spectral plateau}, \lt{i.e.\ a situation in which many coefficients corresponding to sufficiently high polynomial degree $\Vert \miPoly\Vert_1$ terms in the expansion \eqref{eq:truncated} are corrupted and of a similar erroneous magnitude}.
In \Cref{sec:pmisc} we present a method to detect the resulting spectral plateau effect, and embed this plateau detection into the adaptive MISC algorithm to \mg{formulate an automatic strategy named} \plateaumisc{}\mg{, that is robust to intrinsic solver noise}.

