\section{Numerical examples} \label{sec:numerics}
\ah{To illustrate the practical relevance of the proposed \plateaumisc{} method we consider two representative test cases: an \mg{unsteady} advection--diffusion problem \mg{with uncertain coefficients}, and a \mg{parametric} turbulent incompressible Navier--Stokes flow.
The first example is a parametric variant of the well-known \emph{double glazing problem}, originally introduced in \cite{Elman2014,Elman2014b} and based on the benchmark problems from the International Association for Hydraulic Research \cite{Smith1982,Morton1996}.}
% To illustrate a practical engineering need for \plateaumisc{}, a parabolic advection--diffusion PDE test case and a turbulent incompressible Navier--Stokes flow test case are considered in this section.
% The parabolic PDE test case is a parametric formulation of the so-called double glazing problem from \cite{Elman2014b,Elman2014}.
% This itself is derived from \cite{Smith1982,Morton1996} which devised a practical, representative advection--diffusion test problem for the International Association for Hydraulic Research.
% A modified parametric formulation has been used previously in \cite{Kent2023,Kent2023b}.
The second example involves a parametric \ah{version of the \emph{Two Dimensional Wall Mounted Hump (2DWMH)} problem,} proposed by NASA for \ah{turbulence model} validation \cite{NASA2DWMH,Greenblatt2004}.
\ah{This case has been extended} with a parametric suction jet in \cite{Tsiolakis2022} \mg{to control} the reattachment point \ah{of the separated flow downstream of} the `hump'.
% This QoI is known to be challenging to approximate and is of interest from an engineering perspective due to its relation to drag forces.
% \ah{This QoI is a well known challenge in computational fluid dynamics due to its sensitivity and relevance to aerodynamic drag prediction}.

In both {cases}, the fidelity is \bk{controlled} \ah{by a single dimension ($\nModel=1$)}, \ah{while the parametric domain is }two dimensional ($\nY=2)$.
\ah{Although sparse grid methods are typically applied to high-dimensional problems, the focus here is managing the impact of the solver error which is equally significant in these lower-dimensional yet practically relevant settings.}
