Snapshots of the FOM data are collected at an interval of 10, for a total of 200 snapshots. The SPH snapshots are directly stored into a matrix representing a ``Lagrangian" snapshot tensor, and are also mapped onto reference space and stored in matrix form. The POD modes are extracted from both Lagrangian and reference space and the corresponding singular value decay is presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:TGV_LagrangianVReferenceSingularValues}. It is clear to see that singular values decay at a faster rate when the SPH data is mapped onto a reference space. For instance, both Lagrangian and reference space simulation retain 99.9\% of the statistical energy from the FOM with just two modes, $M=2$. However, at five modes, $M=5$, singular values from the reference space retain 99.998\% of the energy, while in Lagrangian space, only 99.96\% is retained. At $M=10$, the reference space retains 99.99998\% and the Lagrangian space retains 99.9992\%. 
%
