\begin{abstract}
Malware developers exploit the fact that most detection models focus on the entire binary to extract the feature rather than on the regions of potential maliciousness. Therefore, they reverse engineer a benign binary and inject malicious code into it. This obfuscation technique circumvents the malware detection models and deceives the ML classifiers due to the prevalence of benign features compared to malicious features. However, extracting the features from the potential malicious regions enhances the accuracy and decreases false positives. Hence, we propose a novel model named \textit{PotentRegion4MalDetect} that extracts features from the potential malicious regions. \textit{PotentRegion4MalDetect} determines the nodes with potential maliciousness in the partially preprocessed Control Flow Graph (CFG) using the malicious strings given by \textit{StringSifter}. Then, it extracts advanced features of the identified potential malicious regions alongside the features from the completely preprocessed CFG. The features extracted from the completely preprocessed CFG mitigate obfuscation techniques that attempt to disguise malicious content, such as suspicious strings. The experiments reveal that the \textit{PotentRegion4MalDetect} requires fewer entries to save the features for all binaries than the model focusing on the entire binary, reducing memory overhead, faster computation, and lower storage requirements. These advanced features give an 8.13\% increase in SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) Absolute Mean and a 1.44\% increase in SHAP Beeswarm value compared to those extracted from the entire binary. The advanced features outperform the features extracted from the entire binary by producing more than 99\% accuracy, precision, recall, AUC, F1-score, and 0.064\% FPR. %Further, it outperforms the state-of-the-art models.
% Malware developers employ reverse engineering techniques on a benign binary to insert malicious code, knowing the malware analysts extract features from the entire binary rather than regions of potential maliciousness. This obfuscation technique bypasses the security measures and deceives the ML classifiers due to the prevalence of benign features compared to malicious features. However, focusing on the regions of potential maliciousness rather than the entire binary improves detection precision and lowers False Positives. Therefore, we propose a novel model named \textit{PotentMalDetector} that focuses on the potential regions of maliciousness to extract advanced features. \textit{PotentMalDetector} identifies the malicious nodes in the Control Flow Graph (CFG) of a binary using top malicious strings given by the \textit{StringSifter}. Then, it extracts advanced features of the identified potential malicious regions alongside the features from the completely preprocessed CFG. The experiments reveal that the \textit{PotentMalDetector} requires fewer entries to save the features for all binaries than the model focusing on the entire binary, reducing memory overhead, faster computation, and lower storage requirements. Further, these advanced features produce 8.13\% and 1.44\% high SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) Absolute Mean and Beeswarm values compared to the features extracted from the entire binary. The Deep Neural Network (DNN) with thirteen layers trained on the advanced features gives better results than features extracted from the entire binary by producing more than 99\% accuracy, precision, recall, AUC, F1-score, and 0.064\% FPR. Further, the \textit{PotentRegion4MalDetect} model outperforms the state-of-the-art models.
% Attackers use reverse engineering techniques on the benign binary to inject the malicious code. Often, malware analysts extract features from the entire binary rather than concentrating on the areas where malicious code is injected. As a result, extracted features are more likely to be categorized as benign than malware since they contain more benign features. This technique bypasses security measures and lowers the chances of finding malware by deceiving Machine Learning (ML) classifiers. Identifying such regions of malicious code and related features enhances the detection accuracy and reduces the False Positive Rate (FPR). Hence, we propose a novel detection model named \textit{PotentRegion4MalDetect} that employs the StringSifter to identify the top malicious strings and use these strings to identify the regions of potential maliciousness in a binary. We extract the advanced features from both, the identified potential malicious regions and the completely preprocessed CFG. The Deep Neural Network (DNN), trained on the extracted advanced features, attains an accuracy of 98.87\%, exceeding the basic features extracted from the whole binary with an accuracy difference of 8.62\%. Even the combination of advanced and basic features trained on DNN achieves an accuracy of 98.7\%, which is 8.45\% higher than the basic features trained on DNN, supporting the importance of the extracted advanced features. We conclude from the comparisons that the proposed PotentRegion4MalDetect model outperforms the state-of-the-art models with feature extraction from the entire binary. 
\end{abstract}

