\section{Conclusion}
\label{section5}
Researchers focus on the entire binary for feature extraction instead of focusing on regions where maliciousness is present. This approach has a downside, i.e., attackers inject malicious code into a benign binary, making the model extract more benign features than malware to bypass the Machine Learning (ML) based approaches. However, we propose a novel approach to extract features from the most potentially malicious regions in a binary along with the features from the completely preprocessed Control Flow Graph (CFG). To see the robustness of the extracted advanced features, we compare these features with the features extracted from the whole binary. We observe a significant performance improvement by including advanced features. The advanced features produce 8.13\% and 1.44\% high SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) Absolute Mean and Beeswarm values compared to the features extracted from the entire binary. We train and test the advanced features on a Deep Neural Network (DNN) with thirteen layers using various combinations of epochs and batch sizes. The combination of 12 epochs and a batch size of 200 yields the best results, achieving an accuracy of 99.95\%, precision of 99.97\%, recall of 99.97\%, AUC of 99.97\%, an F1 score of 99.98\%, and a False Positive Rate (FPR) of 0.063\%. Further, we check with traditional ML models such as LR, DT, RF, SVC, KNN, NB, and LDA and find that RF results exhibit negligible variation compared to the DNN13 model. Finally, we compare the proposed model with state-of-the-art static, dynamic, and hybrid models focused on the whole binary and show why focusing on potential malicious regions is a better approach.% than focusing on the whole binary for feature extraction.
% Researchers focus on the entire binary for feature extraction instead of focusing on regions where maliciousness is present. This approach has a downside, i.e., attackers inject malicious code into a benign binary, making the model extract more benign features than malware to bypass the Machine Learning (ML) based approaches. However, we propose a novel approach to extract features from the most potentially malicious regions in a binary. To see the robustness of the extracted advanced features, we compare these features with the features extracted from the whole binary. We prove the quality of extracted advanced features by further training and comparing the results of the ML models with and without incorporating the advanced features. There is a significant performance improvement by including advanced features. The ML models verify the importance of the extracted advanced features with a maximum accuracy of 98.87\%, F1-Score of 99.1\%, and AUC of 0.9983. In addition, the advanced features trained on DNN13 achieve 8.62\% higher accuracies than the basic features trained on DNN13. Even the combination of advanced and basic features trained on DNN13 achieves 98.7\% accuracy, holding an accuracy gap of 8.45\% from the basic features trained on DNN13. Among all implemented models, the DNN13 with advanced features outperforms the LR, SVC, KNN, RFC, and DTR by 13.97\%, 13.25\%, 2.05\%, 1.80\%, and 0.63\%  more accuracies, respectively. We compare the proposed model with state-of-the-art static, dynamic, and hybrid models focused on the whole binary and show why focusing on potential malicious regions is a better approach than focusing on the whole binary for feature extraction.

