\subsection{Acceleration of Noisy Adjacency Matrix }

The primary drawback of all existing one-round algorithms is that the time complexity during computation is \(O(n^3)\) \cite{imola2021locally, imola2022communication, eden2023triangle}, which makes it challenging to handle counting problems in large-scale graph scenarios. We can significantly reduce the high time complexity by leveraging faster matrix multiplication in our algorithms.

According to research on matrix multiplication \cite{strassen1969gaussian, coppersmith1987matrix, stothers2010complexity, williams2012multiplying, le2014powers, duan2023faster}, the complexity has been reduced from \(O(n^3)\) to \(O(n^{2.371866})\) \cite{duan2023faster}. Although this may seem like a small difference, it becomes particularly significant as \textit{n} increases. For example, when \(n\) is \(10^5\), their running times will differ by a factor of \(10^{5 \times (3-2.371866))} = 10^{3.14067}\), which is approximately 1000 times. We will also verify this in our experiment section.


\section{Subgraph Counting}

In this section, we will design four algorithms based on the properties of the noisy adjacency matrix. Among these, the latter three algorithms achieve more accurate estimations through multiple rounds of queries. However, in second round they face the challenge of providing DP for the data perturbed by GNAM. To address this, we employ a unified idea and method in the next section. Therefore, we use the \textit{second randomizer} serves as a placeholder, the specific implementations for \textit{second randomizer} are detailed in the next section. And we only provide accuracy guarantees without the bias caused by the \textit{ second randomizer} in this section.  






\subsection{Triangle's One-Round Algorithm}

According to Proposition 1, it can be deduced that the elements on the diagonal of the cube of the adjacency matrix \(A\) represent the number of ways a node can return to itself in exactly three steps. Since we assume there are no self-loops in the graph, any path that returns to the starting node in three steps must form a triangle. For each triangle, each node traverses the triangle in two directions, hence the total number of triangles \( f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)=\text{tr}\left( A^3 \right)/ (2 \cdot 3) \).



\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{TriOR}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} \(\epsilon \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\), graph \(G\) .
\Statex \textbf{Output:} Estimate  \(\hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\)  of \(f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\).
\State \(\hat{A} \gets \text{GNAM}(G,\epsilon)\)
\State \textbf{return} \( \text{tr}\left( \hat{A}^3 \right) /6 \)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}



By the second property of Theorem 1, we can conclude that \(\mathbb{E} \left[ \text{tr}\left(\hat{A}^3 \right) \right] = \text{tr}\left(A^3 \right) \). Consequently, by directly computing \( \hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)=\text{tr}\left( \hat{A}^3 \right) /6 \), we can obtain an unbiased estimate of the number of triangles in the graph. We denote this algorithm as Triangle's One-Round Algorithm (TriOR), because there is only one interaction between users and data collector in GNAM. Algorithm 2 presents the detailed implementation of TriOR. Then we show its security guarantees and accuracy bounds:




\begin{theorem}
TriOR provides \(\epsilon\)-edge LDP.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
TriOR provides an unbiased estimate of \(f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\), and its MSE is:
\begin{equation}
\sigma^2\sum_{i=1}^n{\sum_{j=i+1}^n{b_{ij}^{2}}}+\sigma^4(n-2)|E|+\frac{1}{6} \sigma^6 n\left( n-1 \right) \left( n-2 \right)
\end{equation}
And it satisfies: \(\text{MSE} \leqslant O\left(nd_{\max}^3 + n^3\right)\).
\end{theorem}



\subsection{Triangle's Two-Round Algorithm}

If a user \( v_u \) knows the existence of edges between their neighbors, they can count the number of triangles formed by themselves and their neighbors (denoted as \( f^{\bigtriangleup}_u \)). However, the presence or absence of edges among neighbors is private information, which is not accessible to the user. However, users can estimate edge connections between neighbors based on the noisy adjacency matrix, thus inferring the missing third edge in triangles and subsequently estimating \( f^{\bigtriangleup}_u \).

Algorithm 3 shows the Triangle's Two-Round Algorithm (TriTR). In the first round, the data collector obtains a noisy adjacency matrix through GNAM. Subsequently, in the second round, each user downloads the whole noisy adjacency matrix \(\hat{A}\). Each user obtains \( sum_u \) by summing the values between their neighbors in \(\hat{A}\). \( sum_u \) satisfies \(\mathbb{E}[sum_u] = 2 f^{\bigtriangleup}_u\), so \(\mathbb{E}[\frac{1}{6}\sum_{i=u}^n{sum_u}]=f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\). To guarantee \(\epsilon_2\)-edge LDP in the second round, each user process their \(sum_u\) to derive \(\hat{T}_u\) which is then transmitted to the data collector. The collector subsequently sums all \(\hat{T}_u\) and divides the total by six to obtain the final triangle count estimate \( \hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right) \). If the \textit{second randomizer} in line 6 provides \(\epsilon_2\)-edge LDP, we have:


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{TriTR}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Graph \(G\), \(\epsilon_1, \epsilon_2 \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} Estimate \( \hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right) \) of \(f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\).
\Statex \#First round:
\State \(\hat{A} \gets \text{GNAM}(G,\epsilon_1)\)
\Statex \#Second round:
\For{\textbf{each} \textit{user} \(u=1\) to \(n\)}\textbf{:}
    \State \textbf{download} \(\hat{A}\)
    \State \(sum_u \gets \sum_{\left( i,j \right):a_{ui}=a_{uj}=1}{\hat{a}_{ij}}\)
    \State \(\hat{T}_u \gets \textit{second randomizer}\left( sum_u, \epsilon_2 \right) \)
    \State \textbf{upload} \(\hat{T}_u\) to \textit{data collector}
\EndFor
\Statex \textit{data collector} \textbf{do:}
\State \(\hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right) \gets \frac{1}{6}\sum_{i=u}^n{\hat{T}_u}\)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}



\begin{theorem}
TriTR provides \( \left( \epsilon_1+\epsilon_2 \right)\)-edge LDP.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
\(\frac{1}{6}\sum_{i=u}^n{sum_u}\) provides an unbiased estimate of \(f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\), and its MSE is:
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{9}\sigma^2\sum_{i=1}^n{\sum_{j=i+1}^n{b_{ij}^{2}}}.
\end{equation}
And it satisfies: \(\text{MSE} \leqslant O\left(nd_{\max}^3\right)\).
\end{theorem}





\subsection{Triangle's Modified Two-Round Algorithm}




TriTR has achieved a significant improvement in accuracy compared to TriOR. However, it faces the challenge of substantial download costs in the second round, where each user is required to download the entire \(\hat{A}\). To address this issue, we adopt a trade-off approach by increasing the number of noisy edges in each triangle from one to two. This strategy sacrifices a little on accuracy (as shown in Section 7, they achieve similar relative error performance), while significantly reducing the download cost (from the entire matrix to just one column).



\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{TriMTR}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Graph \(G\), \(\epsilon_1, \epsilon_2 \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} Estimate \( \hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right) \) of \(f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\).
\Statex \#First round:
\State \(\hat{A} \gets \text{GNAM}(G,\epsilon_1)\)
\State \textit{Data collector} \textbf{calculate:} \(\hat{B} \gets \hat{A}^2\)
\Statex \#Second round:
\For{\textbf{each node} \(u=1\) to \(n\)}\textbf{:}
    \State \textbf{download} the \(u\)-th column of \(\hat{B}\)
    \State \(sum_u \gets \sum_{i: a_{ui}=1}{\hat{b}_{iu}}\)
    \State \(\hat{T}_u \gets \textit{second randomizer}\left( sum_u, \epsilon_2 \right) \)
    \State \textbf{upload} \(\hat{T}_u\) to \textit{data collector}
\EndFor
\Statex \textbf{Data collector do:}
\State \(\hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right) \gets \frac{1}{6}\sum_{u=1}^n{T_u}\)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{f}^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}





Algorithm 4 shows the Triangle's Modified Two-Round Algorithm (TriMTR). The data collector computes the squared matrix \(\hat{B} = \hat{A}^2\) in the first round. In the second round, each user \(v_u\) downloads the \(u\)-th column of \(\hat{B}\), sums the neighbor-associated entries in \(sum_u\), applies privacy protection to generate \(\hat{T}_u\), and uploads it to the data collector. According to the first property of Theorem 1, \(\mathbb{E}[\hat{b}_{iu}] = b_{iu}\). Therefore, if \(a_{ui} = 1\), then \(\mathbb{E}[a_{ui}\hat{b}_{iu}] = a_{ui}b_{iu}\), which represents the number of triangles formed by the edge \((v_u, v_i)\). And thus \(\mathbb{E}[sum_u] = 2f^{\bigtriangleup}_u\). Consequently, \(\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{6}\sum_{u=1}^n sum_u\right] = f^{\bigtriangleup}(G)\). TriTR holds the following guarantees:






\begin{theorem}
TriMTR provides \( \left( \epsilon_1+\epsilon_2 \right)\)-edge LDP.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
\(\frac{1}{6}\sum_{u=1}^n sum_u\) provides an unbiased estimate of \(f^{\bigtriangleup}\left( G \right)\), and its MSE is:
\begin{equation}
\frac{4}{9}\sigma^2\sum_{i=1}^n{\sum_{j=i+1}^n{b_{ij}^{2}}}+\frac{1}{9}\sigma^4(n-2)|E|.
\end{equation}
And it satisfies: \(\text{MSE} \leqslant O\left(nd_{\max}^3 + n^2d_{\max}\right)\).
\end{theorem}





\subsection{Quadrangle's Two-Round Algorithm}

User \( v_u \) provides the two edges connecting themselves and their neighbors, and then utilizes \( \hat{B} = \hat{A}^2 \) to derive the noisy two-step relationships among their neighbors. This enables the estimation of the number of quadrangles they are part of (denoted as \( f^{\Box}_u \)). Following this rationale, we designed Quadrangle's Two-Round Algorithm (QuaTR) shown in Algorithm 5.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{QuaTR}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Graph \(G\), \(\epsilon_1, \epsilon_2 \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} Estimate \( \hat{f}^{\Box}\left( G \right) \) of \(f^{\Box}\left( G \right)\).
\Statex \#First round:
\State \(\hat{A} \gets \text{GNAM}(G,\epsilon_1)\)
\State \textit{Data collector} \textbf{calculate:} \(\hat{B} \gets \hat{A}^2\)
\Statex \#Second round:
\For{\textbf{each node} \(u=1\) to \(n\)}\textbf{:}
    \State \textbf{download} \(\hat{B}\)
    \State \(sum_u \gets \sum_{\left( i,j \right):a_{ui}=a_{uj}=1}{\left( \hat{b}_{ij}-1 \right)}\)
    \State \(\hat{Q}_u \gets \textit{second randomizer}\left( sum_u, \epsilon_2 \right) \)
    \State \textbf{upload} \(\hat{Q}_u\) to \textit{data collector}
\EndFor
\Statex \textbf{Data collector do:}
\State \(\hat{f}^{\Box}\left( G \right) \gets \frac{1}{8}\sum_{u=1}^n{\hat{Q}_u}\)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{f}^{\Box}\left( G \right)\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}









The implementation of QuaTR is similar to that of TriTR, with modifications only in lines \(4, 5\) and \(8\). The change in line 5 is necessary because user \(v_u\) provides two edges \(v_u \to v_i\) and \(v_j \to v_u\), while the remaining paths of the form \(v_i \to v_k \to v_j\) are provided by \(\hat{b}_{ij}\). Since \(\mathbb{E}[\hat{b}_{ij}]=b_{ij}\) includes the path \(v_i \to v_u \to v_j\), which cannot form a quadrangle with \(v_u \to v_i\) and \(v_j \to v_u\), it is necessary to minus 1 to exclude this invalid path. Since each of the four vertices of a quadrangle will traverse the quadrangle from two distinct directions, we need to change line \(8\) by dividing by \(8\). Similarly, we have the following guarantees:





\begin{theorem}
QuaTR provides \( \left( \epsilon_1+\epsilon_2 \right)\)-edge LDP.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
\(\frac{1}{8}\sum_{u=1}^n{sum_u}\) provides an unbiased estimate of \(f^{\Box}\left( G \right)\), and its MSE is:
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{4}\sum_{i=1}^n{\sum_{j=i+1}^n{c_{ij}^2\sigma^2}}+\frac{1}{16}(n-2)\sum_{i=1}^n{\sum_{j=i+1}^n{b_{ij}^2\sigma^4}}.
\end{equation}
And it satisfies: \(\text{MSE} \leqslant O\left( n d_{\max}^5 + n^2d_{\max}^3 \right)\).
\end{theorem}



\subsection{Subgraph Algorithms and Matrix Powers}




The intuitive ideas behind the design of these algorithms are illustrated in Figure 5. The black edges represent edges within the real graph, while the red edges denote noisy edges in \(\hat{A}\). The powers of the noisy adjacency matrix correspond to the number of noisy edges within paths of a given length. 



Moreover, the variance of each algorithm can be expressed in terms of the powers of the adjacency matrix, which further reveals a profound connection between the design of the algorithm and the matrix. The Frobenius Norm of a matrix \(A\) is: 

\begin{equation}
\|A\|_F = \left( \sum_{i=1}^{m} \sum_{j=1}^{n} |a_{ij}|^{2} \right)^{1/2}
\end{equation}


%This is shown on~\ref{sec:Variance in Matrix Form}. Additionally, the directed graph versions of these algorithms can be found at ~\ref{sec:Directed Graph Application}.








In this analysis, we focus solely on the variance terms presented in Section 5. By defining the \(0\)-th power of any matrix \(M \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}\) as the identity matrix \(I_n\) and ignoring coefficients, we observe the following patterns:

\begin{equation}
\mathbb{V}(\text{TriTR})=O(\sigma^2 \|A^2\|^2_F)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{V}(\text{TriMTR})=O(\sigma^2 \|A^2\|^2_F + n \sigma^4 \|A^1\|^2_F)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{V}(\text{TriOR})=O(\sigma^2 \|A^2\|^2_F + n \sigma^4 \|A^1\|^2_F + n^2 \sigma^6 \|A^0\|^2_F)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{V}(\text{QuaTR})=O(\sigma^2 \|A^3\|^2_F + n \sigma^4 \|A^2\|^2_F)
\end{equation}


%In the equation of variance, the sum of the powers of \( n \) and \( A \) always equals to the step length of the cycle (3-cycle, 4-cycle) minus one, and the power of \(\sigma\) always equals 2 plus twice the power of \(n\).





\section{Second Round's Randomizer}
The two-round algorithms need to provide edge LDP for the data randomized in the first round, which makes it challenging to determine \(\Delta f\) and thus prevents directly using the Laplace mechanism to achieve \(\epsilon_2\)-edge LDP. In this section, we present our solution to this problem, including the key ideas and implementation details.

%Furthermore, it is imperative to provide additional privacy protection in the second round; failure to do so not only prevents the achievement of \(\epsilon_2\) edge LDP but also risks the exposure of the user's edge privacy.









\subsection{Differential Privacy on Randomized Data}




\textbf{Key idea.} Due to the randomness of \(\Delta f\), we can utilize the distribution of \(\Delta f\) to determine its \(\beta\) quantile. This approach allows us to constrain \(\Delta f\) within a smaller range, thereby providing \(\epsilon_2\)-edge LDP.

We employ the clamp function to bound \(\Delta f\). Let \(\kappa\) denote the bound set for \(\Delta f\). We set \(\kappa\) equal to the larger absolute value between the upper and lower \(\beta\) quantiles of \(\Delta f\). Then, using clamp function: \(\text{clamp}(\Delta f, \kappa) = \text{max}(\text{min}(\Delta f, +\kappa), -\kappa)\) to restrict the change. After clamping, the \(\Delta f=\kappa\), then we can provide \(\epsilon_2\)-edge LDP by Laplace mechanism.





If \(\beta\) is too small, it can reduce the error introduced by clamping but may result in a larger \(\Delta f=\kappa\), thereby introducing excessive noise in the second round. Conversely, if \(\beta\) is too large, the error caused by clamp operation will increase. Therefore, it is crucial to select an appropriate \(\beta\). Then the key challenge is to determine the appropriate \(\Delta f\) based on \(\beta\).







