\subsection{Implementation}


The majority of real networks holds \(d_{\max} \gg d_{\text{avg}}\), if all nodes employ the same \(\Delta f\), this would result in excessive noise being added to the majority of nodes. To address this issue, for each node \(v_u\), we utilize its noisy degree value \(\tilde{d}_u\) to provide a more tailored and tighter bound \(\Delta f_u\). We denote the privacy budget allocated for obtaining \(\tilde{d}_u\) as \(\epsilon_0\). According to Proposition 1, this "customized" assignment of \(\Delta f_u\) to each user still ensures that the overall system provides \((\epsilon_0 + \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2)\) edge LDP.



We use GraphProjection (showed in Algorithm 6) to obtain noisy degrees \(\tilde{d}_u\). To prevent an excessive number of edges from being removed in the graph, we introduce a parameter \(\alpha\) to \(\tilde{d}_i\). This ensures that edges of nodes with \(d_{i} \leq \alpha\) remain intact, while nodes with \(d_{i} \geq \alpha\) have a probability of \(\frac{1}{2}e^{-\frac{\alpha}{\epsilon_0}}\) for edge removal.


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{GraphProjection}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Adjacency list \(\mathbf{a}_i\), degree \(d_i\), \(\epsilon_0\), \(\alpha\).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} processed adjacency list \(\mathbf{a}'_i\), noisy degree \(\tilde{d}_i\).
\State  \(\tilde{d}_i =\lfloor \alpha+\max \left\{ d_i +  \text{Lap}\left(\frac{1}{\epsilon_0}\right), 0\right\}\rfloor \)
\State Remove \(d_i- \tilde{d}_i  \) neighbors randomly if \(\tilde{d}_i < d_i\) to get \(\mathbf{a}'_i\).
\State \textbf{return} \(\mathbf{a}'_i\), \(\tilde{d}_i\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}



Let \(\text{Nei}_u\) be the index set of the user \(v_u\)'s neighbor. \(\Phi^{-1}: \left[0,1\right] \to \mathbb{R}\) is the inverse distribution function of the standard normal distribution. Then using Algorithm 7 replaces lines 4-5 in TriTR, Algorithm 8 replaces lines 5-6 in TriMTR, and Algorithm 9 replaces lines 5-6 in QuaTR. Each user \(v_u\), bounds the change contributed by any one of \(v_u\)'s neighbors to \(sum_u\) within \((- \Delta f, + \Delta f)\). The \(sum_u\)'s difference between neighbor lists \(\mathbf{a}_u\) and \(\mathbf{a}_u'\) is kept below \(\Delta f\). Consequently, adding \(\text{Lap}(\Delta f / \epsilon_2)\) noise achieves \(\epsilon_2\)-edge LDP. Let \(\lambda_u\) denote the first input of each clamp function. Algorithms 7-9 possess the following probabilistic guarantee to constrain the impact of the clamp function on the final accuracy.

\begin{theorem}
It can be approximated that:
\begin{equation}
\Pr\left[\lambda_u >\Delta f_u\right] < \beta
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\Pr\left[\lambda_u <-\Delta f_u\right] < \beta
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}



\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{TriTR's Second Randomizer}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Noisy adjacency matrix \(\hat{A}\); user \(u\)'s noisy degree \(\tilde{d}_u\), and it's neighbor set \(\text{Nei}_u\); \(\epsilon_2\).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} \(\hat{T}_u\).
\State \(\Delta f_u \gets \Phi^{-1}(1 - \beta) \cdot \sqrt{\tilde{d}_u\sigma^2} + \tilde{d}_u \)
\State \(sum_u \gets \sum_{i \in \text{Nei}_u}{\text{clamp}(\sum_{j \in \text{Nei}_u, j < i}{\hat{a}_{ij}}, \Delta f_u)}\) 
\State \(\hat{T}_u \gets 2 (sum_u+  \text{Lap}(\Delta f_u/\epsilon_2) )  \)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{T}_u\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{TriMTR's Second Randomizer}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Noisy two-step matrix \(\hat{B}\)'s \(u\)-th column \(\hat{\mathbf{b}_u}\); user \(u\)'s noisy degree \(\tilde{d}_u\), and it's neighbor set \(\text{N}_u\); \(\epsilon_2\).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} \(\hat{T}_u\).
\State \(\Delta f_u \gets \Phi^{-1}(1 - \beta) \cdot \sqrt{(n-2)\sigma^4+\left(\tilde{d}_u+\tilde{d}_\max\right)\sigma^2} + \tilde{d}_u \)
\State \(sum_u\gets \sum_{i\in N}{\text{clamp}(\hat{b}_{iu}, \Delta f_u)}\)
\State \(\hat{T}_u \gets sum_u + \text{Lap}(\Delta f_u/\epsilon_2) \)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{T}_u\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{QuaTR's Second Randomizer}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Noisy two-step matrix \(\hat{B}\); user \(u\)'s noisy degree \(\tilde{d}_u\), and it's neighbor set \(\text{N}_u\); \(\epsilon_2\).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} \(\hat{Q}_u\).
\State \(\Delta f_u \gets \Phi^{-1}(1 - \beta) \cdot \sqrt{ \tilde{d}_u ( 2d_{\max} \sigma^2 + (n-2)\sigma^4 ) } + \tilde{d}_u(d_{\max}-1) \)
\State \(sum_u \gets \sum_{i \in \text{Nei}_u}{\text{clamp}(\sum_{j \in \text{Nei}_u, j < i}{\left(\hat{b}_{ij}-1 \right)}, \Delta f_u)}\) 
\State \(\hat{Q}_u \gets 2 (sum_u + \text{Lap}(\Delta f_u/\epsilon_2) ) \)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{Q}_u\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}






























Because we use the Central Limit Theorem in the proof of Theorem 11, the term "approximate" appears in the theorem. Next, we present the safety and accuracy guarantees for each two-round algorithm.

\begin{theorem}
TriTR provides (\(\epsilon_0 + \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2\))-edge LDP, and if we use RR or Laplace mechanism in GNAM, the total MSE satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\text{MSE}\leqslant O\left(\frac{nd_{\max}^3}{\epsilon_1^2}+ \frac{|E|}{\epsilon_1^2\epsilon_2^2}+n\frac{d_{\max}^2}{\epsilon_2^2} \right)
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
TriMTR provides (\(\epsilon_0 + \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2\))-edge LDP, and if we use RR or Laplace mechanism in GNAM, the total MSE satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\text{MSE}\leqslant O\left(\frac{nd_{\max}^3}{\epsilon_1^2}+ \frac{n^2d_{\max}}{\epsilon_1^4} + \frac{n^2}{\epsilon_1^4\epsilon_2^2}+n\frac{d_{\max}^2}{\epsilon_2^2} \right)
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
QuaTR provides (\(\epsilon_0 + \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2\))-edge LDP, and if we use RR or Laplace mechanism in GNAM, the total MSE satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\text{MSE}\leqslant O\left(\frac{nd_{\max}^5}{\epsilon_1^2}+ \frac{n^2d_{\max}^3}{\epsilon_1^4}   +  \frac{n^2d_\max}{\epsilon_1^4\epsilon_2^2}+n\frac{d_{\max}^4}{\epsilon_2^2}\right)
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}






\section{Theoretical Analysis}


%In this section, we will present the convergence of relative errors for the two-round triangle counting algorithms and provide a theoretical utility comparison between our algorithms and existing ones. This aims to establish reasonable expectations for the experimental results discussed in the next section.


In this section, we theoretically analyze the proposed algorithms to guide the experimental evaluation. Section 7.1 shows the convergence properties on relative error for both two-round triangle counting algorithms. Section 7.2 develops and analyzes a noisy degree-based estimation algorithm for 2-stars, complete with theoretical performance guarantees. Section 7.3 presents a theoretical comparison between our proposed algorithms and existing algorithms in the literature.



\subsection{Convergence on Relative Err}

The specific expression of MSE has already been provided in the previous sections, so we now focus on RE.

To analyze the Relative Error, we will employ the \textbf{Jensen's Inequality}: \(\mathbb{E}\left[ |X-\mathbb{E}\left[X\right]| \right] \leqslant \sqrt{\mathbb{V}\left( X \right)}\). In this way, we can bounded the Absolute Error (ABE). Next, dividing ABE by \( f^{\bigtriangleup}(G) \), we can bound RE.

Because the graph may not have any triangles, such as in a bipartite graph, which would make RE meaningless. Therefore, we need to make some constraints on the graph structure. Assuming that the \textit{clustering coefficient} (equals to \( \frac{3 \times \# \text{triangles}}{\#\text{2-stars}} \)) exists, the relative error of TriTR and TriMTR satisfies:

\begin{theorem}
If the clustering coefficient of the graph exists, the RE of TriTR satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\text{RE} \leqslant O\left( \frac{1}{\epsilon_1d_{\text{avg}}} + \frac{1}{\epsilon_1\epsilon_2 \sqrt{n}d_{\text{avg}}^{\frac{3}{2}} } + \frac{1}{\epsilon_2\sqrt{n}d_{\text{avg}}} \right)\text{;} 
\end{equation}
The RE of TriMTR satisfies:
\begin{equation} 
\text{RE} \leqslant O\left( \frac{1}{\epsilon_1 d_{\text{avg}}}
+ \frac{1}{\epsilon_1^2 d_{\text{avg}}^{\frac{3}{2}}}
+ \frac{1}{\epsilon_1^2 \epsilon_2d_{\text{avg}}^2}
+ \frac{1}{\epsilon_2 \sqrt{ n } d_{\text{avg}}}
\right)\text{.}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

According to Theorem 15, we can conclude that the denser the network (larger \(d_{\text{avg}}\)), the smaller the relative error. This also draws our attention to the fact that, given an overall privacy budget \(\epsilon = \epsilon_0 + \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2\), appropriately allocating more to \(\epsilon_1\) can reduce the overall error. Additionally, the accuracy of TriTR and TriMTR slightly improves as the graph size increases. 




