\subsection{Two-Star Counting}

Through GraphProjection, we obtained \(\tilde{d}_i\), which not only serves to mitigate the errors introduced in the second round but also provides the count of 2-stars within the graph. This can be specifically achieved by implementing Algorithm 10.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{2STAR}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Adjacency list \(\mathbf{a}_u\), degree \(d_u\), \(\epsilon_0\), \(\alpha\).
\Statex \textbf{Output:} estimation of 2-stars in graph \(G\).
\For{\textbf{each node} \(u=1\) to \(n\)}\textbf{:}
    \State \(\tilde{d}_u = \text{GraphProjection}(\mathbf{a}_u,d_u,\epsilon_0,\alpha) \)
    \State \textbf{upload} \(\hat{d}_u\) to \textbf{data collector}
\EndFor
\Statex \textit{data collector} \textbf{do:}
\State \(\hat{f}^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right) \gets \sum_{u=1}^n{\left[(\tilde{d}_u-\alpha)(\tilde{d}_u-\alpha-1)-2/\epsilon_0^2\right]} \)
\State \textbf{return} \(\hat{f}^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right)\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}





We cannot provide its exact theoretical MSE, but we can explain the inspiration behind this algorithm and offer an approximate theoretical guarantee:

\begin{theorem}
Let \(\hat{d}_u=d_u+\text{Lap}(1/\epsilon_0)\), \(f^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right)\) denote the true number of \(2\)-star in graph \(G\), then the estimator:
\begin{equation}
\hat{f}^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right) \gets \sum_{u=1}^n{\left[\hat{d}_u(\hat{d}_u-1)-2/\epsilon_0^2\right]} 
\end{equation}
has the following properties:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\hat{f}^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right)]=f^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\text{MSE}(\hat{f}^{\text{2-star}}\left( G \right))=\frac{8}{\epsilon_0^2}\sum_{u=1}^n{d_u^2}-\frac{16}{\epsilon_0^2}|E|+\frac{2}{\epsilon_0^2}n+\frac{20}{\epsilon_0^4}n
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
RE \leqslant O(\frac{1}{\sqrt{\#2\text{-star}}})
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

Algorithm 10 does not align fully with the above theorem because the GraphProjection step requires ensuring \(\tilde{d}_u \geq \alpha\). However, the discrepancy should be minimal. This is because the majority of 2-stars in the graph are contributed by nodes with higher degrees, and for these nodes, \(\tilde{d}_u - \alpha\) approximately equals \(\hat{d}_u\).


This implies that in practical implementations, the two-round algorithm operates as follows: In the first round, each user applies GraphProjection to obtain \(\tilde{d}_u\) then applies GNAM to generate $\tilde{\mathbf{a}}_u$, both $\tilde{d}_u$ and $\tilde{\mathbf{a}}_u$ are uploaded to the data collector. This allows the collector to simultaneously derive the 2-star count estimate before proceeding with the second round for triangle or quadrangle estimation. If downloading the entire matrix is acceptable in the second round, users can download \(\hat{B}\) and upload $\hat{T}_u$ and $\hat{Q}_u$ concurrently, allowing simultaneous estimation of triangle, quadrangle, and 2-star counts.






\subsection{Theoretical Comparison}



Table 2 presents the state-of-the-art subgraph counting algorithms under edge-LDP: \(\text{RR}_{\bigtriangleup}\) \cite{imola2021locally}, \(\text{2R-Large}_{\bigtriangleup}\) \cite{imola2022communication}, \(\text{ARR}_{\bigtriangleup}\) \cite{imola2022communication}, \(\text{2R-Small}_{\bigtriangleup}\) \cite{imola2022communication}, \(\text{Wshuffle}_{\bigtriangleup}\) \cite{imola2022differentially}, \(\text{Wshuffle}_{\Box}\) \cite{imola2022differentially}, \(\text{LocalLap}_{2\star}\) \cite{imola2021locally}
and our algorithms: TriOR, TriTR, TriMTR, QuaTR, 2STAR.







\(\text{ARR}_{\bigtriangleup}\) with a sampling probability \(p_0 = O(n^{-\frac{1}{3}})\) in edge-sampling, the time complexity can be reduced to \(O(n^2)\). \(\text{2R-Small}_{\bigtriangleup}\) (which is \(\text{ARROneNS}_{\bigtriangleup}\) in \cite{imola2022communication}) with a sampling probability \( p_0 = \frac{e^{\varepsilon} + 1}{e^{\varepsilon} \sqrt{n}} \), the \(\text{Cost}_{DL}\) can be reduced to \(O(n \log n)\) \cite{imola2022differentially}. It should be noted that the term \(\log(n)\) in \(\text{Cost}_{DL}\) arises from representing the node indices in binary form, since the \(n\) nodes require \(\log(n)\) bits. The transmission of our algorithms uses floating-point numbers. If we treat the number of bits required for floating-point numbers and for representing \(n\) nodes as constants, then the \(\text{Cost}_{DL}\) for both \(\text{2R-Small}_{\bigtriangleup}\) and TriMTR is \(O(n)\).

Among one-round algorithms, TriOR has achieved an enhancement in accuracy while reducing the time complexity. Although the theoretical analysis of TriTR does not indicate improvement in precision, experiments in next section demonstrate that TriTR has attained the highest accuracy among all algorithms. This discrepancy occurs because TriTR introduces less cumulative noise in the second round compared to \(\text{2R-Large}_{\triangle}\). When the \(\text{Cost}_{DL}\) is O(n), TriMTR exhibits superior precision compared to \(\text{2R-Small}_{\bigtriangleup}\). QuaTR surpasses \(\text{Wshuffle}_{\Box}\) in terms of accuracy. The 2STAR provides higher accuracy than \(\text{LocalLap}_{2\star}\) since \(\text{LocalLap}_{2\star}\) requires first knowing or estimating \(d_{\max}\), after which each user computes their local 2-star count, adds \(\text{Lap}(d_{\max}/\epsilon)\) noise, and uploads the result. This noise addition leads to larger MSE than 2STAR.





