\begin{abstract}
% Data imbalance in classes across clients is a challenge for federated learning, which leads the local models to have different partitions of features spaces and harms the generalization capability of the global model. Existing methods either follow the knowledge distillation approach that uses the global model to guide the training of consistent local models in clients, or introduce a procedure to calibrate the local models before global aggregation on the server side. However, these approaches overlook the negative impact introduced by the long-tailed representation learning, resulting in the aggregation of ill-posed local models.  To address this issue, this paper presents a prototype-guided cross-silo consistent representation learning method, termed FedCRL, which uses data prototypes obtained from both the lage-scale pre-trained model and the local data to improve the learning and aggregation of local models. Specifically, FedCRL first uses class textual representation learned from pre-trained CLIP to regularize the local representation learning in the in-silo representation calibration (ISRC) module, which facilitates the learning of a consistent feature space between clients on imbalanced data. Subsequently, the cross-silo consistent representation learning (CSCRL) module leverages statistical knowledge learned from client-side to calibrate class-aware regions to enrich the feature space. Finally, the CSCRL module maps features from diverse spaces into a unified space and obtains the global model by learning consistent representations for cross-source features. Experimental results from three datasets verified that FedCRL effectively reduces the differences between diverse source spaces, which results in superior performance compared to existing methods.










% Cross-silo feature alignment offers a promising solution in federated learning, which aims to learn a unified feature space between data sources. Existing methods either utilize knowledge distillation to facilitate consistency in the optimization objectives among clients, or fine-tune the model on the server to fit different feature spaces. However, they both ignore the negative impact of data biases, such as imbalanced data hinders forming effective features for classes with the minority of samples. To address this issue, this paper proposes a prototype-guided cross-silo consistent representation learning method, termed FedCRL, which improves local representation learning on imbalanced data and further aligns class-aware feature regions to learn generalized model on the server. Specifically, FedCRL first uses in-silo representation calibration (ISRC) module to regularize the representation learning, which can improve the discriminability of features on imbalanced data and lay a solid foundation for cross-silo consistent representation learning (CSCRL). Subsequently, the CSCRL module uses knowledge transfer to calibrate class-aware regions to enrich the feature space. Moreover, the CSCRL module further learns the unified representations across sources and make the final decision in a weighted manner to reduce the interference of outliers. Experimental results from three datasets verified that FedCRL effectively reduces the differences between diverse source spaces, which results in superior performance compared to existing methods.





% Cross-silo feature alignment offers an effective solution in federated learning to handle data heterogeneity by learning a unified feature space. Existing methods either utilize prototypes as the regularizer to guide feature learning on the client or learn a generalized mapping on the server to align feature spaces from different sources. However, they both ignore the negative impact of data biases, such as imbalanced data hinders forming effective features for classes with the minority of samples. To address this issue, this paper proposes a prototype-guided cross-silo consistent representation learning method, termed FedCRL, which balances local feature space on imbalanced data and further aligns class-related regions on the server to learn generalized model. Specifically, FedCRL first uses in-space distribution balancing (ISRC) module to enhance the feature learning, which can improve the discriminability of features on imbalanced data and lay a solid foundation for cross-silo distribution consistency learning (CSCRL). Subsequently, the CSCRL module uses knowledge transfer to calibrate class-aware regions to enrich the feature space. Moreover, the CSCRL module further learns the unified representations across sources and make the final decision in a weighted manner to reduce the interference of outliers.
% Experimental results from three datasets verified that FedCRL effectively reduces the differences between diverse source spaces, which results in superior performance compared to existing methods.

Federated Class Incremental Learning (FCIL) aims to collaboratively process continuously increasing incoming tasks across multiple clients. Among various approaches, data replay has become a promising solution, which can alleviate forgetting by reintroducing representative samples from previous tasks. However, their performance is typically limited by class imbalance, both within the replay buffer due to limited global awareness and between replayed and newly arrived classes. To address this issue, we propose a \underline{c}lass-wise \underline{b}alancing \underline{d}ata \underline{r}eplay method for FCIL (\methodname{}), which employs a global coordination mechanism for class-level memory construction and reweights the learning objective to alleviate the aforementioned imbalances. Specifically, \methodname{} has two key components: 1) the global-perspective data replay module reconstructs global representations of prior task in a privacy-preserving manner, which then guides a class-aware and importance-sensitive sampling strategy to achieve balanced replay; 2) Subsequently, to handle class imbalance across tasks, the task-aware temperature scaling module adaptively adjusts the temperature of logits at both class and instance levels based on task dynamics, which reduces the model’s overconfidence in majority classes while enhancing its sensitivity to minority classes. Experimental results verified that \methodname{} achieves balanced class-wise sampling under heterogeneous data distributions and improves generalization under task imbalance between earlier and recent tasks, yielding a 2\%-15\% Top-1 accuracy improvement over six state-of-the-art methods.





\end{abstract}

