} 
\label{fig4}
\end{figure*}
Figure \ref{fig4} gives a sensitivity analysis of \methodname{} with respect to temperature and sample weighting hyperparameters. Overall, temperature scaling and sample re-weighting help mitigate class imbalance, but model performance varies considerably with different hyperparameter settings. The model achieves better overall performance when $\omega_{\text{old}} = 1.1$, $\omega_{\text{new}} = 0.9$, $\tau_{\text{old}} = 0.9$, and $\tau_{\text{new}} = 1.1$. This is because slightly higher weight and temperature for previous-task samples help retain old knowledge, while lower weight and higher temperature for newly arrived samples reduce overfitting and improve adaptation.
However, inappropriate hyperparameter choices may harm performance. For instance, a large $\tau_{\text{new}}$ (e.g., 2.0) leads to overly smooth predictions, reducing discrimination among newly arrived classes. These results emphasize the need for proper tuning to ensure balanced learning.

