Furthermore, scores with our tokenizer exhibit much higher variance than the baseline (except for French-Italian), suggesting that this tokenization approach may be less reliable than standard SentencePiece.




\subsection{Language Modeling}

Figure~\ref{fig:ppl} demonstrates that \usp achieves improved perplexity per byte across all language pairs and vocabulary sizes compared to the baseline. Interestingly, this improvement does not correlate with tokenization scores: the \usp model with the worst intrinsic evaluation scores achieves the lowest perplexity in language modeling.
Table~\ref{tab:eval_ppl_bytes} shows that bilingual training with both the source and target language improves the perplexity per byte on the target language in low-resource languages.

