\begin{document}

\title{Logarithmic Depth Decomposition of Approximate Multi-Controlled Single-Qubit Gates Without Ancilla Qubits}

\author{
        Jefferson D. S. Silva\orcidlink{0000-0002-2063-6140},
        Adenilton J. da Silva\orcidlink{0000-0003-0019-7694}}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The synthesis of quantum operators involves decomposing general quantum gates into the gate set supported by a given quantum device. Multi-controlled gates are essential components in this process. In this work, we present improved decompositions of multi-controlled NOT gates with logarithmic depth using a single ancilla qubit, while also reducing the constant factors in the circuit depth compared to previous work. We optimize a previously proposed decomposition of multi-target, multi-controlled special unitary SU(2) gates by identifying the presence of a conditionally clean qubit. Additionally, we introduce the best-known decomposition of multi-controlled approximate unitary U(2) gates without using ancilla qubits. This approach significantly reduces the overall circuit depth and CNOT count while preserving an adjustable error parameter, yielding a more efficient and scalable solution for synthesizing large controlled-unitary gates. Our method is particularly suitable for both NISQ and fault-tolerant quantum architectures. All software developed in this project is freely available.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Quantum circuits, multi-controlled gates, conditionally clean qubits.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Quantum computing has emerged as a transformative technology capable of revolutionizing cryptography, optimization, and material science~\cite{shor1999polynomial, zhou2020quantum, cao2019quantum, bauer2020quantum, preskill2018quantum}. However, implementing quantum algorithms on near-term quantum devices, known as Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices~\cite{preskill2018quantum}, poses significant challenges due to limited qubit coherence times and high error rates. One of the critical tasks in quantum circuit design is the efficient decomposition of complex quantum gates into elementary gates, such as single-qubit gates and controlled-NOT (CNOT) gates, to minimize circuit depth and gate count. 

Multi-controlled gates play a central role in quantum circuit design and have motivated a wide range of studies on their decomposition and optimization~\cite{barenco_1995, shende2006synthesis, maslov2016advantages, iten2016quantum, he2017decompositions, adenilton2022linear, vale2023circuit, claudon2024polylogarithmic, silva2024linear, nie2024quantum, khattar2025rise}.
A prominent example is the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate~\cite{barenco_1995}, which applies an $X$ gate to a target qubit conditioned on the state of $n$ control qubits. The efficient implementation of such gates is crucial for a wide range of applications, including quantum error correction~\cite{inada2021measurement}, quantum arithmetic~\cite{remaud2025ancilla}, and quantum simulation~\cite{morgado2021quantum}. 

Recent advancements in quantum circuit optimization have introduced the concept of \textit{conditionally clean ancillae}, which are qubits that are in a known state conditioned on the state of other qubits~\cite{nie2024quantum}. This concept has been leveraged to reduce the gate count and depth of quantum circuits and the total number of ancillary qubits required in the circuit design, particularly in the decomposition of $n$-qubit Toffoli gates with $O(log(n))$ depth.

Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise} achieves a decomposition of the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate with depth $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$. This decomposition has a gate count of $8n-12$ using two clean ancillae or $16n-32$ using two dirty ancilla qubits. 

This approach represents an improvement over previous methods, which either required a larger number of ancillary qubits or resulted in higher gate counts.

In parallel, research on approximate decompositions of multi-controlled single-qubit gates has shown promise in reducing the complexity of quantum circuits. In Ref.~\cite{silva2024linear}, the authors proposed a method for approximating $n$-controlled single-qubit gates with size and depth $\mathcal{O}(n)$ without ancilla qubits, and with size $\mathcal{O}(n)$ and depth $\mathcal{O}(\log{n})$ using $n - 3$ ancilla qubits, significantly reducing the CNOT gate count while maintaining a controlled approximation error. In Ref.~\cite{claudon2024polylogarithmic}, the authors introduced an approximate $n$-controlled single-qubit gate decomposition with an adjustable error parameter $\epsilon$, achieving a depth of $\mathcal{O}(\log{n^3} \cdot \log{(1/\epsilon)})$ and size $\mathcal{O}(n \log{n^4})$ without ancilla qubits. These approaches are particularly relevant for NISQ devices, where the trade-off between circuit complexity and approximation error can be carefully managed to enhance the overall performance of quantum algorithms.

In this work, we present an improved CNOT decomposition based on~\cite{khattar2025rise}, reducing both the ancillary count to one and the circuit depth. Furthermore, we develop multi-controlled, multi-target $X$ and $SU(2)$ operators with $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ depth, enhancing the approximate decomposition of multi-controlled $U(2)$ operators proposed by Silva et al.~\cite{silva2024linear}. Our construction achieves ancilla-free operation in the final circuit with $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ depth and lower CNOT cost. 

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II reviews related work, including the decomposition of multi-controlled gates and the use of conditionally clean ancillae. Section III introduces our proposed method for optimizing the decomposition of $n$-qubit Toffoli gates using conditionally clean ancillae, achieving logarithmic depth and linear CNOT count, using one (clean or dirty) ancilla qubit. Section IV presents our method for decomposing multi-controlled, multi-target $X$ and $SU(2)$ gates, and approximate multi-controlled $U(2)$ gates without ancilla qubits. Section VI concludes with a discussion of potential future research directions.

\section{Related Works}

\subsection{Toffoli Gate up to a Diagonal Gate}

In the context of quantum circuit decomposition, the Toffoli gate plays a crucial role due to its ability to efficiently implement controlled operations. Lemma 6 of~\cite{iten2016quantum} provides a decomposition of the Toffoli gate up to a diagonal gate using single-qubit rotations. These results are further elaborated in~\cite{maslov2016advantages}, where the decomposition is expressed in terms of $T$ gates as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:relative_phase}. This relative-phase Toffoli decomposition is particularly useful for reducing the number of CNOT gates in quantum circuits.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{images/rp.pdf}}
\caption{Implementation of a Toffoli gate up to a diagonal gate, using $H, T$ and CNOT gates. Adapted from~\cite{maslov2016advantages}.}
\label{fig:relative_phase}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Multi-Controlled, Multi-target SU(2) decomposition}
 Ref.~\cite{silva2024linear} presents a decomposition of multi-controlled, multi-target $SU(2)$ gates that achieve linear depth and CNOT cost. The corresponding circuit design is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:mcmt_su2}. In this decomposition, the control qubits are divided into two groups: $k_1 = \lceil n/2 \rceil$ and $k_2 = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$. The decomposition works as follows: when all control qubits are active, the circuit implements $W_i = \sigma_x \cdot A_i \cdot \sigma_x \cdot A_i^\dagger \cdot \sigma_x \cdot A_i \cdot \sigma_x \cdot A_i^\dagger$, where $A_i \in SU(2)$. However, if any control qubit is inactive, the circuit implements the identity operation on all target qubits. The resulting circuit requires at most $16n + 8m - 32$ CNOT gates and has a depth of $32n + 8m - 52$.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/mcmt_su2.pdf}}
\caption{Circuit design scheme for multi-controlled, multi-target $SU(2)$ gates, as described by Silva et al.~\cite{silva2024linear}, where $W_i \in SU(2)$ has at least one real-valued diagonal, and $t = {1, 2, \ldots , m}$.} \label{fig:mcmt_su2} 
\end{figure}

\subsection{Approximate Decompositions}

The decomposition of approximate multi-controlled $U$, where $U \in \mathrm{U}(2)$ gates, introduced by~\cite{silva2024linear}, follows a structured approach that uses controlled $R_x$ rotations to implement multi-controlled $U$ gates with linear CNOT count and depth, Fig.~\ref{fig:mcu_approx}. In the exact case~\cite{adenilton2022linear}, when all control qubits are active, the circuit applies successive roots of $U$ to the target qubit, ensuring that their product results in $U$. To obtain an approximate version, the lowest-index root is removed, introducing a bounded approximation error. Fixing this error truncates the number of control qubits required, $n_b$, limiting circuit growth. To maintain scalability, the complexity is shifted toward the circuit's center, where a multi-target $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ operator with a single control qubit is used. Instead of a single control, additional control qubits $n_e$ can be employed, leading to an improved design with $n = n_b + n_e$ control qubits.

For a given target error $\epsilon$, the number of control qubits, $n_b$, remains fixed, while the decomposition of multi-controlled, multi-target $W$ gates is used to manage the growth of the control qubits, as described in~\cite[Theorem 3]{silva2024linear}. The required truncation depth scales as:
\begin{equation}\label{scale_nb}
    n_b \geq \log_2\left(\frac{|\theta|}{\arccos(1 - \epsilon^2 / 2)} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\theta$ depended on the original $U$~\cite{silva2024linear} operator. 

\subsection{Conditionally Clean Ancillae}

Ref.~\cite{nie2024quantum} introduced the concept of conditionally clean ancillae, which are qubits whose state is guaranteed to be on a known basis (e.g. $\ket{0}$) only when conditioned on specific control qubits. For example, if a clean qubit $a$ stores the logical AND of controls $\{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_k\}$, then $a = \ket{1}$ implies $\bigwedge_{i=1}^k c_i = 1$. This property allows $c_1, \dots, c_k$ to act as a temporary workspace in subsequent computations without additional ancillas, provided that operations are restricted to the subspace where $a = \ket{1}$. This technique reduces ancillary dependency while preserving reversibility, making it a powerful tool for optimizing multi-controlled gates. Ref.~\cite{nie2024quantum} proposes a decomposition of CNOT gates with $O(log(n))$ depth.  

\begin{figure}[t]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/mcu_aprrox.pdf}}
\caption{Approximate decomposition of a multi-controlled $U(2)$ gate with six control qubits. The six control qubits are split into two groups: the extra control qubits $\{e_1, e_2\}$ and the base control qubits $\{b_1, b_2, b_3, b_4\}$. The blocks highlighted in red correspond to the decomposition of a multi-controlled, multi-target $SU(2)$ operation. Adapted from the method described in~\cite{silva2024linear}. The $R_x$ gates are labeled with their corresponding rotation angles $\pm\pi/2^k$, for $k \in \mathbb{N}$.}
\label{fig:mcu_approx}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Log-Depth Decomposition of Toffoli Gates with Conditionally Clean Ancillae}

Based on Ref~\cite{nie2024quantum}, Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise} proposes a decomposition for $C^n X$ (the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate)  
with $2n - 3$ Toffoli gates and $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ depth using 2 clean ancillae, and $4n -8$ Toffoli gates with $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ depth using 2 dirty ancillae. The approach recursively partitions controls into blocks, using intermediate results as conditionally clean ancillae to apply gates in parallel,  where intermediate controls are reused as a workspace, reducing both depth and the ancillary count compared to quadratic depth methods~\cite{khattar2025rise}.  

Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise} operates through five sequential phases. The protocol first employs a Toffoli gate to initialize two conditionally clean qubits using one clean ancilla, then constructs a ladder of $n - \log n - 2$ Toffoli gates to accumulate the AND of remaining controls into $\log n$ qubits. It subsequently applies their linear-depth Toffoli decomposition to these accumulated controls with an additional clean ancilla, followed by mirroring the initial ladder structure for uncomputation, and concludes by restoring the original ancilla state. 

For dirty ancillae implementations, the protocol extends this framework by appending a second application of the AND accumulation, core computation, and partial uncomputation steps after the initial five-phase cycle, increasing the total Toffoli count from $2n-3$ to $4n-8$ while preserving the $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ depth characteristic.

\section{n-qubit Toffoli gate with reduced depth}

This section presents an optimized decomposition for the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate, building upon the approach proposed by Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise}. 
Unlike the original construction, which requires two clean ancilla qubits, our decomposition achieves the same functionality using only one clean ancilla, minimizing resource overhead. To store the control information, we leverage conditionally clean ancilla qubits and optimize their parallel utilization, matching the depth of the relative-phase circuit. This improvement enhances scalability and suitability for near-term quantum devices, where reducing both ancilla count and depth is critical for mitigating error rates.

To construct an $n$-qubit Toffoli gate with $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ depth with one clean ancilla qubit, we divide the circuit into five steps. Fig.~\ref{fig:mcx9_log} illustrates the circuit design for a $9$-qubit Toffoli gate. 

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/mcx9.pdf}}
\caption{$n$-qubit Toffoli gate with one clean ancilla qubit with depth \(\mathcal{O}(\log{n})\). 
The white diamond in $ctrl[8]$ is used as a conditionally clean ancilla qubit for step 3. This conditionally clean ancilla allows the recursive application of the logarithmic depth decomposition.
}
\label{fig:mcx9_log}
\end{figure}

In the first step, we use a Toffoli gate to generate two conditionally clean ancilla qubits. As previously noted, due to the circuit's symmetry, the Toffoli gate can be implemented up to a diagonal gate as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:relative_phase}.  


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/primivite_circ.pdf}}
\caption{Ladder of $X$ and Toffoli gates decomposed into a ladder of Toffoli gates, up to a diagonal gate, where $A = \sigma_x \cdot H \cdot T$, and $B =T^\dagger \cdot H$. Illustrated for 3 targets. The overall circuit has a depth of 7.}
\label{fig:primitive_circuit}
\end{figure}

Step 2 adds a $\log$-depth ladder of $ n-\log (n) - 2$ Toffoli gates (up to a diagonal gate) in the remaining $n-2$ control qubits. Fig.~\ref{fig:primitive_circuit} details how to implement each ladder block using a relative phase. Since the gates commute on the target qubits, the overall depth remains the same as that of a single Toffoli gate, up to a diagonal gate. This step stores the AND of all remaining $n-2$ qubits in $\log(n)$ control qubits.  

Step~3 introduces a $\log(n+1)$-qubit Toffoli gate. To implement this step, we recursively apply the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate with logarithmic depth, replacing the linear decomposition proposed by Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise}. By selecting one control qubit (not involved in storing the AND of the $n-2$ qubits from Step~2) as a conditionally clean qubit, we apply $X$ gates to this qubit before and after the recursive operation. This approach reduces the auxiliary qubit requirement from two (as in Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise}) to one. Steps~4 and~5 invert Steps~2 and~1, respectively. This circuit design yields the following theorem:  

\begin{theorem}\label{teo:log_mcx}
    Constructing a quantum circuit that implements an $n$-qubit Toffoli gate using one clean ancilla, with a total of $6n-6$ CNOT gates and depth $\mathcal{O}(\log{n})$ is possible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}

We replace the original linear-depth decomposition in Step~3 with the logarithmic-depth variant proposed by~\cite{khattar2025rise}, which preserves the same CNOT count due to the equivalent gate complexity of both approaches. Furthermore, we reuse one control qubit, selected from the $n-\log{n}-2$ controls not used in the final AND accumulation (Step~2), as a conditionally clean ancilla for Step~3. This strategy takes advantage of the flexibility introduced by our modified recursive partitioning method. As a result, we obtain $    6n - 6 \,\mathrm{CNOTs}$.

To analyze the depth, we note that Steps~1 and~5 each contribute a constant depth of 7. As described in Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise}, Steps~2 and~4 contribute a depth of $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$. Step~3, however, contributes a depth of $\mathcal{O}(\log{\left(\log{n}\right)})$. Therefore, the overall circuit depth is $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$.


\end{proof}

Step~3 of the decomposition described by Theorem~\ref{teo:log_mcx} adds a depth of $\mathcal{O}\left(\log \left({\log {n}}\right)\right)$ to the circuit, rather than the $\mathcal{O}(\log {n})$ depth reported in previous work~\cite{khattar2025rise}. This improvement effectively reduces the depth of the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate decomposition.

The comparative analysis in Fig.~\ref{fig:experiment_two} demonstrates the efficiency of our method in reducing circuit depth for $n$-qubit Toffoli gates. 
We implemented the decompositions with qiskit version 2.0.0 and used the qiskit transpile function with optimization level equal to 3.
We achieved logarithmic depth scaling described by $25.5903 \cdot \log_2(n) - 12.1237$, significantly outperforming the Ref~\cite{khattar2025rise} ($29.3675 \cdot \log_2(n) - 28.2752$).

This shallower depth is particularly valuable for near-term quantum devices, where shorter circuits help mitigate error accumulation and improve operational fidelity. Furthermore, the logarithmic model's consistency (validated through adjusted $R^2$ metrics and residual analysis) confirms the robustness of the approach.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/log_mcx_depth.pdf}}
\caption{Comparative analysis of the depth of the $n$-qubit Toffoli gate using one clean ancilla. The blue dashed line represents the decomposition scheme proposed by Khattar and Gidney~\cite{khattar2025rise}, while the black solid line illustrates our method, which achieves reduced depth and CNOT count. The magenta dots represent the data points from the Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise} method, and the green squares represent our data.}
\label{fig:experiment_two}
\end{figure}

To construct a version of the circuit that uses a dirty ancilla, we extend the clean ancilla circuit by repeating steps 2, 3, and 4 alongside it. This construction leads to the following corollary:

\begin{corollary}
It is possible to construct a quantum circuit that implements an $n$-qubit Toffoli gate using one dirty ancilla, with a total of $12n-18$ CNOT gates and depth $\mathcal{O}(\log{n})$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Logarithmic-Depth Implementations of Multi-Controlled Single Qubit Gates}

Building upon our $\log{n}$-depth decomposition of $n$-qubit Toffoli gates, we extend this approach to implement multi-controlled single-qubit operations. We present exact decompositions for arbitrary $W \in SU(2)$ gates, and approximate implementations for general $U \in U(2)$ operators. Each case maintains the logarithmic depth scaling while adapting the control structure through optimized ancilla management and gate synthesis. These results enable efficient implementations of quantum algorithms requiring controlled rotations or unitary transformations, particularly in scenarios where circuit depth is the limiting resource.

\subsection{Logarithmic-Depth Decomposition of Multi-Controlled, Multi-Target single qubit gates}

Multi-controlled multi-target gates arise in various quantum algorithms and state preparation routines~\cite{zindorf2024efficient, silva2024linear, tomesh2024quantum, gui2024spacetime, rosa2025optimizing}, particularly when the same unitary operation needs to be conditionally applied to multiple qubits. A straightforward adaptation of single-target decompositions to the multi-target case would require applying the same multi-controlled operation on each target sequentially, a cascade of decompositions.

In contrast, our multi-target approach leverages the fact that the control qubits are identical for every target, enabling a parallel implementation. Instead of executing a chain of multi-controlled operations, a single circuit is constructed that conditionally applies the desired unitary simultaneously to all target qubits when all control qubits are active. In this section, we propose a logarithmic-depth decomposition strategy for $n$-controlled $m$-target $W$ gates. Our approach employs an optimized implementation of a multi-controlled multi-target Toffoli gate and extends the linear-depth scheme of Ref.~\cite{silva2024linear} into a log-depth decomposition. As a result, the method achieves both a low CNOT gate count and reduced depth, making it particularly suitable for near-term quantum devices with limited coherence times.

We implement the $m$-target $X$ gate by applying a pair of CNOT gates to each target qubit, one before and one after the $n$-controlled $X$ operation, following the approach described in Ref.~\cite{wille2013improving}. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:mtx}, the circuit architecture consists of cascades of CNOT gates flanking an $n$-qubit Toffoli gate. A key optimization is achieved by rearranging the control qubits to group commuting CNOT operations into parallelizable layers, each containing at most $2^p$ gates. This design enables the construction of an $n$-controlled $m$-target $X$ gate ($C^n \bigotimes_{i=1}^m X_i$) with an optimal depth scaling of $\mathcal{O}(\log{n} + \log{m})$.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/mtx.pdf}}
\caption{Log-depth multi-target $X$ gates scheme, illustrated for 7 targets. The circuit design features a cascade of CNOT gates surrounding an $n$-qubit Toffoli gate, with control qubits strategically arranged to enable parallelization.}
\label{fig:mtx}
\end{figure}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:mcmtx}
    An $n$-controlled $m$-target Toffoli gate can be constructed using one clean ancilla qubit, with a total of $6n + 2m - 8$ CNOT gates and a depth of $\mathcal{O}(\log{n} + \log{m})$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    To implement the $n$-controlled part, we apply Theorem~\ref{teo:log_mcx}, which requires $6n - 6$ CNOT gates and has a depth of $\mathcal{O}(\log{n})$. For the $m$-target part, we add 2 CNOT gates for each $m-1$ additional targets. Thus, the total number of CNOT gates becomes:
    \[
        6n + 2m - 8 \,\mathrm{CNOTs}.
    \]
    To compute the circuit depth for the $m$-target part, we arrange commutative groups of CNOT gates in powers of two on both sides of the $n$-controlled Toffoli gate. Each side forms $L = \log(m)$ layers, resulting in a total of $2L$ layers. Therefore, the total depth is:
    \[
        \mathrm{Depth:} \, \mathcal{O}(\log{n} + \log{m}).
    \]
\end{proof}

Regarding the depth in the multi-target part, this decomposition remains particularly advantageous, even with a single control qubit. Although a single control qubit only requires a cascade of CNOT gates, effectively halving the CNOT count in the target part, the resulting depth is linear. In contrast, for $m$-target CNOT gates, Lemma~\ref{lem:mcmtx} achieves a depth of $\mathcal{O}(\log{m})$.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/log_mcmt_su2.pdf}}
\caption{Logarithmic-depth decomposition of an $n$-controlled $m$-target $SU(2)$ gate. The white diamond in $k_2$ is used as a conditionally clean ancilla qubit
for $k_1$-qubit $m$-target Toffoli gate. The design allows parallelization of gate blocks to minimize depth, using $k_2$ as a conditionally clean ancilla qubit.}
\label{fig:log_mcmt_su2}
\end{figure}

For $n$-controlled $m$-target $W$ decomposition, we retain the same circuit design as in Fig.~\ref{fig:mcmt_su2}. However, a few modifications are sufficient to achieve logarithmic depth. We can do $k_1 = n-1$ and $k_2 = 1$ and replace the $\{k_1, k_2\}$-qubit Toffoli gate in the circuit of Fig.~\ref{fig:mcmt_su2} by substituting Lemma 8 of Ref.~\cite{iten2016quantum} with our optimized version, described in Lemma~\ref{lem:mcmtx}, which reduces both the CNOT cost and depth. In this configuration, the qubit in $k_2$ is used as a conditionally clean ancilla by the $k_1$-qubit Toffoli gates, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:log_mcmt_su2}.


\begin{theorem}\label{teo:mcmtsu2}
    It is possible to construct a quantum circuit that implements an $n$-controlled $m$-target $SU(2)$ gate using at most $12n+8m-30$ CNOT gates, with depth $\mathcal{O}(\log{n} + \log{m})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    Consider an $n$-controlled $m$-target $SU(2)$ gate, $C^n \bigotimes_{i=1}^m W_i$, where $W_i \in SU(2)$. First, we divide the control qubits into two groups: $k_1 = n-1$ and $k_2 = 1$.  

    For the first target ($m=1$), we apply Theorem~\ref{teo:log_mcx}, which requires two $k_1$-qubit Toffoli gates acting on the target qubit while using $k_2$ as a conditionally clean ancilla qubit. These two Toffoli gates contribute $2\cdot [6k_1 - 6]$ CNOTs. Additionally, 2 CNOT gates act on the target, controlled by $k_2$.  

    As described in Lemma~\ref{lem:mcmtx}, for subsequent $m-1$ targets, $8\cdot(m -1)$ CNOT gates are required. Summing all contributions, we obtain:  
    \[
        12n+8m-30 \,\mathrm{CNOTs}.
    \]

    For the circuit depth, the gates $A_i$ and $A_i^\dagger$ ($i = 1, 2, \dots, m$) are applied in parallel, along with the $X$ gates, which prepare a conditionally clean qubit in $k_2$. Additionally, two CNOT gates with control in $k_2$ are applied, contributing a depth of 6.  

    The remaining depth contributions come from the two $k_1$-qubit Toffoli gates and the four $m$-target $X$ gates as described by Lemma~\ref{lem:mcmtx}. Therefore, the overall depth is:  
    $
        \mathrm{Depth:} \, \mathcal{O}(\log{n} + \log{m}).
    $
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/experiment1_su2.pdf}}
\caption{Comparative CNOT cost for decomposing multi-controlled $W \in SU(2)$ gates. The black dashed line shows the method from~\cite[Theorem 2]{silva2024linear}, the blue solid line represents our decomposition (Theorem~\ref{teo:mcmtsu2}), and the red stars indicate CNOT counts from transpiled Qiskit circuits.}
\label{fig:mcmtsu2}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:mcmtsu2} presents a comparative analysis of the CNOT cost for different multi-controlled $W$ gate decompositions. The black dashed line represents the CNOT count predicted by the decomposition scheme in~\cite[Theorem 2]{silva2024linear}, while the blue solid line shows the theoretical CNOT cost of our approach, as described in Theorem~\ref{teo:mcmtsu2}. The red star markers correspond to the CNOT counts obtained by transpiling our decomposition using Qiskit and counting the resulting CNOT gates. As shown in the graph, our method achieves a significantly lower CNOT cost compared to the previous linear-depth strategy and aligns closely with the expected analytical behavior. This supports the effectiveness of our log-depth construction for practical circuit synthesis.


\subsection{Logarithmic-Depth Decomposition of Approximate Multi-Controlled U(2) gates}

 Theorem 3 of Ref.~\cite{silva2024linear} uses a linear depth multi-controlled, multi-target $SU(2)$ decomposition, for optise their approximate multi-controlled $U(2)$ decomposition. 
 We propose replacing this multi-controlled, multi-target $SU(2)$ decomposition with our newly optimized version, which features logarithmic depth and lower CNOT cost.

\begin{theorem}\label{teo:last}
    An approximate decomposition of a $n$-controlled $U$ gate ($C^n U$), where $U \in \mathrm{U}(2)$, can be implemented up to an error $\epsilon$ with a CNOT count upper bound of $4(n_b - 1)^2 + 24n - 8n_b -4$ and $\mathcal{O}(\log{n})$ depth, where $n_b$ is a fixed base number of control qubits that depends on $\epsilon$ as in Eq.~\eqref{scale_nb}, and $n \geq n_b + 5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    The proof follows a similar structure to Theorem 3 of Ref.~\cite{silva2024linear}, but with a key difference: instead of using Theorem 2 of Ref.~\cite{silva2024linear} to account for $(n_e + 1)$-controlled $(n_b - 1)$-target $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ gates, we use our improved version described in Theorem~\ref{teo:mcmtsu2}. 

    By applying Theorem~\ref{teo:mcmtsu2}, we account for $2[12(n_e + 1) + 8(n_b - 1) - 30]$ CNOTs. Adding these to the $4(n_b-1)^2$ CNOTs from the multi-controlled $U^{1/2^j}$ gates and expressing the result in terms of the total number of controls $n$ and $n_b$ (where $n = n_b + n_e$), the total CNOT count becomes:
    \[
        4(n_b - 1)^2 + 24n - 8n_b -52 \quad \text{CNOTs}.
    \]

    For the depth, note that the growth of the circuit depends on the extra control qubits $n_e$, while $n_b$ remains fixed up to an error $\epsilon$. Thus, asymptotically, the depth matches that of Theorem~\ref{teo:mcmtsu2}, i.e.: 
    $
     \mathcal{O}(\log{n})
    $ depth.
\end{proof}


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/experiment2_u2.pdf}}
\caption{Comparative analysis of the CNOT cost in the approximate decomposition of multi-controlled $U = X$ gate using different methods. The blue solid line represents the analytical CNOT cost described by Theorem~\ref{teo:last}, the purple star markers indicate our experimental results, and the black dashed line corresponds to the decomposition from~\cite[Theorem 3]{silva2024linear}.}
\label{fig:mcu2}
\end{figure}


Fig.~\ref{fig:mcu2} presents a comparative analysis of the CNOT cost in the approximate decomposition of multi-controlled $U = X$ gates using different methods. The cyan solid line represents the analytical CNOT cost predicted by our decomposition described in Theorem~\ref{teo:last}, while the purple star markers indicate the computational experimental result. The black dashed line corresponds to the decomposition proposed in~\cite[Theorem 3]{silva2024linear}. Both methods exhibit linear CNOT cost concerning the number of control qubits $n$, but our approach achieves a cost of $\mathcal{O}(24n)$ compared to $\mathcal{O}(32n)$ in the method by Silva et al. Furthermore, our decomposition also features logarithmic depth, making it especially suitable for near-term quantum devices with limited coherence times.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}


This work presents optimized decompositions for multi-controlled and multi-target quantum gates with improved resource efficiency. Building upon Ref.~\cite{khattar2025rise} $n$-qubit Toffoli gate design, we develop an enhanced version that maintains logarithmic depth scaling in the number of controls while reducing the constant factors, and lowers the ancilla requirement to just one (either clean or dirty). The improvement is based on two modifications: (1) replacing Step~3 of the original decomposition with a logarithmic-depth variant that exhibits lower depth, and (2)  using one of the control qubits as conditionally clean, thereby enabling the ancilla reduction. These optimizations achieve significant resource savings while preserving exact functionality.
%
We design an $n$-controlled $m$-target $X$ gate with depth $\mathcal{O}(\log n + \log m)$ using this optimized Toffoli construction as a foundation. This represents a significant asymptotic improvement over previous implementations. 

Future research should explore two key optimizations for $n$-qubit Toffoli gates. First, we propose investigating whether conditionally clean qubits can enable relative-phase $n$-qubit Toffoli implementations that eliminate auxiliary qubits, potentially reducing qubit overhead and gate depth. Second, when these gates are employed as subroutines in larger circuits, their CNOT count may be further optimized through gate cancellation or shared ancilla techniques across multiple Toffoli operations. These improvements could significantly enhance the practicality of near-term quantum algorithms relying on controlled operations.


